(2683,	1,	'2011-09-01 23:34:06',	'0000-00-00 00:00:00',	'[caption id=\"attachment_2618\" align=\"alignright\" width=\"250\" caption=\"Matt Stohrer\"]<img class=\"size-full wp-image-2618\" title=\"matthew-stohrer\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/matthew-stohrer.jpg\" alt=\"Matt Stohrer\" width=\"250\" height=\"389\" />[/caption]\r\n\r\nFor many of us, the topic of saxophone equipment is one that we know little to nothing about. So when it comes to taking care of our horn, having it fixed, or buying a new one altogether,<strong> it\'s easy to get lost</strong> in the morass of esoteric information that only a few people seem to <em>really</em> possess.\r\n\r\nAs many of you know, Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever. has recently been blessed with <a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?s=is+it+worth+buying+a+pro+model\">two info-crammed interview articles </a>with renown saxophone repair guru, <strong>Matt Stohrer</strong> of saxophone repair and sales company,<strong> <a href=\"https://stohrermusic.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Storher Music</a></strong>.\r\n\r\nBecause of Matt\'s voracious passion for the saxophone as well as his beloved craft of repairing, restoring, and setting up horns, we\'ve decided to share that passion in the form of<strong> a new column where you, the reader, emails Matt, the guru your questions about anything saxophone-equipment related.</strong>\r\n\r\nSome topics that Matt can help you with include:\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>A problem that your instrument is having</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>How to tell if your instrument is having a problem</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>Buying a saxophone (new or used)</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>How much a horn should cost</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>General information on the mechanics of the saxophone</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>How to make sure that your horn is being properly taken care of by your own repairperson</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\nReally, <strong>anything related to saxophone gear is fair game</strong>, so just fire away and we\'ll do everything we can to get you Matt\'s answer as well as your name and a link to your website if you wish.\r\n\r\nNow, of course you can go to your local repairperson and get their opinions on this stuff, and there are many highly qualified and honest repairfolk out there. But in many cases, there\'s a good chance that <strong>what they tell you will be influenced by their own desire to acquire your business</strong> as either a customer purchasing their repair services or instruments - or both. What we\'re looking to provide is <strong>accurate and unbiased information</strong> that will help you get the most out of your saxophone journey.\r\n\r\nWe\'re really excited to answer your questions and hopefully help inform the entire saxophone community on things that <strong>they would have been left to guess about before</strong>.\r\n\r\nSo what are you waiting for? Let\'s hear from you today and <strong>get your saxophone issues sorted out</strong> right quick!',	'Introducing New Column: \"Ask a Repairman\"',	'',	'draft',	'open',	'open',	'',	'',	'',	'',	'2011-09-01 23:34:06',	'2011-09-02 06:34:06',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=2683',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
(2695,	1,	'2012-07-19 06:00:51',	'2012-07-19 13:00:51',	'<strong><img class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-2709\" title=\"david-sanborn\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local/wp-content/uploads/david-sanborn.jpg\" alt=\"David Sanborn\" width=\"550\" height=\"198\" />Ever notice that there\'s something missing from your favorite Benny Carter, Bird, or Coleman Hawkins recordings?</strong> Well, yes, they are conspicuously missing a good <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melodica\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">melodica</a> solo, but that\'s not what I\'m talking about.\r\n<h3>Things Ain\'t What they Used to Be</h3>\r\nThough it\'s not completely absent from their playing, these jazz pioneers used the altissimo register far less than what we hear in the saxophone heros of today.  Where the practical range of of the horn used to extend up to F above the staff, <strong>nowadays we\'re hearing players go miles above that high F</strong> into notes that we don\'t even have official fingerings for.\r\n\r\nWhile classical saxophone repertoire has employed the altissimo register for decades, there are many of us for whom the buck stops at palm key F. However, when we listen to altissimo masters such as <strong>Michael Brecker, Chris Potter, </strong>and the unparalleled altissimo god,<strong> Lenny Picket</strong>, it can be a bit overwhelming to imagine ourselves moving around an area of the horn that is not only foreign territory for our fingers, but a virtual otherworld when it comes to tone production.\r\n\r\nSo what\'s the best way to get started on this journey towards the saxophone stratosphere?\r\n<h3>The Easy Way</h3>\r\n<em><strong>One note, one week at a time.</strong></em>\r\n\r\nIf you start out trying to rip through your major and minor scales above that palm key F, you\'re probably going to find your entire body twisting in knots of frustration and despair. The cliche, \"Rome wasn\'t built in a day\" isn\'t a cliche for nothing. To start building your own upper register \"Rome,\" one of the best strategies is to <strong>extend the range of your instrument very slowly and gradually</strong>.\r\n<h3>Height at the End of the Tunnel</h3>\r\nSo let\'s say that palm key F is the top of your range. No problem - all we need to do is take the F# above that note, and <strong>incorporate that F# into all of our long tones, scales, arpeggios, and even our improvised solos.</strong> For example, when you practice your chromatic scale, go all the way up to the F#. When you practice your F# major scale, you can now go up two octaves. When you play your F# minor arpeggios, there\'s no longer any need to stop at C# - keep going all the way up to that F# ceiling. <em>I think you get the picture.</em>\r\n\r\nWe do this for a week, maybe even two until it becomes comfortable for us. Just like anything else in music, the point isn\'t to learn it quickly, but to do learn it so that it becomes <strong>natural and intuitive</strong> for us to use what we\'re learning in as musical a manner as possible.\r\n\r\nThe next week is \"G week.\" Incorporate the altissimo G into your practice routine the same way you did with the F#. And up we go week after week. Before you know it, <strong>you\'ll be breaking glass with the best of \'em.</strong>\r\n\r\nNow, the art of playing in the altissimo register is a whole other topic in and of itself, so here are some resources on the site to help you get started:\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//11-tips-for-improving-your-altissimo/\">11 Tips for Improving Your Altissimo</a></li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//12-tips-for-hitting-that-altissimo-g-like-a-pro/\">12 Tips for Hitting that Altissimo G Like a Pro</a></li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//intro-to-saxophone-overtones-part-1-what-are-they\">Intro to Saxophone Overtones Part 1 – What are They?</a></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<h4><strong>So go forth my brave saxofriends and conquer the ever-elusive altissimo register - one note, one week at a time. </strong></h4>',	'Altissimo the Easy Way',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'altissimo-the-easy-way',	'',	'\nhttps://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//11-tips-for-improving-your-altissimo/',	'2018-02-09 13:29:47',	'2018-02-09 19:29:47',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=2695',	0,	'post',	'',	6,	0),
