(187,	904,	'Bret Pimentel',	'bpimentel+wordpress@gmail.com',	'https://www.bretpimentel.com',	'24.119.138.10',	'2010-12-09 17:27:04',	'2010-12-10 01:27:04',	'Hey Doron,\r\n\r\nThanks for the kind words and the link. I\'m glad you like my Fingering diagram builder, and I hope your readers will, too.\r\n\r\nIncidentally, they can jump straight to the saxophone fingering chart here:\r\n<a href=\"https://fingering.bretpimentel.com/#/saxophone/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://fingering.bretpimentel.com/#/saxophone/</a>\r\n\r\nBret',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 6.0; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	0),
(188,	904,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-09 17:28:18',	'2010-12-10 01:28:18',	'Ahh, OK, perfect, thanks for sharing the link to the actual diagram builder. :)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	187,	1),
(193,	723,	'Sam Sadigursky',	'sadigursky@gmail.com',	'http://www.SamSadigursky.com',	'207.38.131.12',	'2010-12-11 10:34:33',	'2010-12-11 18:34:33',	'\"What am I afraid of?\" - Ask this in your practice session and be honest with yourself. You\'ll unlock the doors to many new things.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.5; en-US; rv:1.9.1.1) Gecko/20090715 Firefox/3.5.1',	'',	0,	0),
(194,	723,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-12 12:24:11',	'2010-12-12 20:24:11',	'Good philosophy for life as well! :)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; en-US; rv:1.9.2.13) Gecko/20101203 Firefox/3.6.13',	'',	193,	1),
(195,	928,	'Bret Pimentel',	'bpimentel+wordpress@gmail.com',	'https://www.bretpimentel.com',	'209.147.243.180',	'2010-12-13 08:49:38',	'2010-12-13 16:49:38',	'Benny Carter\'s <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B000003NA0?ie=UTF8&amp;tag=bretpimewood-20&amp;linkCode=as2&amp;camp=1789&amp;creative=390957&amp;creativeASIN=B000003NA0\" rel=\"nofollow\"><i>Further Definitions</i></a> doesn\'t meet all your qualifications for a blowing session, as it\'s pretty heavily arranged. But it\'s definitely a fascinating chance to hear four different approaches by four great saxophonists: Carter, Charlie Rouse, Coleman Hawkins, and a young Phil Woods. One of my favorites!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	0),
(196,	928,	'Bret Pimentel',	'bpimentel+wordpress@gmail.com',	'https://www.bretpimentel.com',	'209.147.243.180',	'2010-12-13 08:52:22',	'2010-12-13 16:52:22',	'Oops, I was so anxious to talk about <i>Further Definitions</a> that I missed it, right there near the top of your list. Great minds...',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	0),
(198,	928,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.254.160.62',	'2010-12-13 14:51:44',	'2010-12-13 22:51:44',	'Yeah, totally on the same page with you Bret, that\'s gotta be one of the most legendary recorded saxophone summits ever. \r\n\r\nGreat minds indeed... :)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	196,	1),
(200,	928,	'Marc Plotkin',	'marc@marcplotkin.com',	'https://www.marcplotkin.com',	'74.64.49.105',	'2010-12-13 19:53:00',	'2010-12-14 03:53:00',	'I\'m not sure if it was ever officially released, but there\'s a mind-blowing bootleg you can probably find on some torrent of Coltrane ON ALTO, with Gene Ammons live in Japan. Almost made me quit when I heard how equally godly Trane sounded on alto.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.224 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	0),
(201,	928,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-13 22:13:52',	'2010-12-14 06:13:52',	'Wow, that\'s crazy, never heard of that one, woud love to hear it since I think that hearing that Coltrane approach on an alto would be trippy and awesome!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	200,	1),
(204,	632,	'Adam Niewood',	'aniewood@yahoo.com',	'https://www.niewood.com',	'69.86.184.22',	'2010-12-14 11:37:36',	'2010-12-14 19:37:36',	'Doron,\r\n\r\n Thanks for your interest in what I do, and for interviewing me...   This is a cool resource.\r\n\r\n Sincerely,\r\n\r\n Adam Niewood',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.5; en-US; rv:1.9.2.13) Gecko/20101203 Firefox/3.6.13',	'',	0,	0),
(205,	632,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-14 13:12:05',	'2010-12-14 21:12:05',	'My pleasure Adam, always happy to spread the word about talented folks such as yourself. :)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	204,	1),
(209,	1016,	'Rick Hirsch',	'rick@hirschmusic.com',	'http://www.hirschmusic.com',	'68.232.115.161',	'2010-12-15 10:49:53',	'2010-12-15 16:49:53',	'There\'s something fundamentally wrong about that, and not because Kenny G happens to be the saxophonist in the video.  \r\n\r\nHe\'s an easy target for many, as you stated — so let\'s not go there.  Despite what other will say, I think that Kenny G has done good in terms bringing wider awareness of \"jazzy\" instrumental music, and the saxophone in particular.\r\n\r\nBut, about this vid...',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_5_8; en-us) AppleWebKit/533.19.4 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.0.3 Safari/533.19.4',	'',	0,	0),
(210,	1016,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-15 10:57:21',	'2010-12-15 18:57:21',	'Oh yeah, I agree, that\'s why I put the disclaimer. For Kenny, that\'s the music that speaks to him, as there\'s no way that he would have gotten to where he is by playing music he hates and is only doing for the money. I know a lot of people who are sincerely moved by his playing, so right there he\'s doing something that a lot of \"jazz geniuses\" are unable to do.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	209,	1),
(211,	1016,	'Bret Pimentel',	'bpimentel+wordpress@gmail.com',	'https://www.bretpimentel.com',	'209.147.243.180',	'2010-12-15 13:51:29',	'2010-12-15 21:51:29',	'Still not quite in the same category as Coltrane, but here\'s evidence that he is (or at least <i>was</i>) capable of more than one style:\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SqoA3O5hZ4E\r\n\r\nI have to admit, I would steal some of those licks. I never would have guessed the guy playing the lick at 3:45 was the same guy who recorded \"Songbird.\"\r\n\r\nIncidentally, I was in Beijing, China a couple of years ago and met some saxophonists from the band at Tsingua University. (There isn\'t a music department there--I think they were engineering students.) I asked what saxophonists they like, and they immediately said Kenny G. I asked who else, and they couldn\'t name any others.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	0),
(212,	1016,	'ericdano',	'ericdano@gmail.com',	'',	'99.23.162.4',	'2010-12-15 15:52:19',	'2010-12-15 23:52:19',	'I think this is faked. What the drummer is hitting is not what I hear......and kenny\'s hands don\'t match what he\'s playing.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-us) AppleWebKit/533.19.4 (KHTML, like Gecko)',	'',	0,	0),
(213,	1016,	'ericdano',	'ericdano@gmail.com',	'',	'99.23.162.4',	'2010-12-15 15:57:41',	'2010-12-15 23:57:41',	'Actually, most the YouTube comments say that this is Trane......and that the video is actually some other song. It\'s like the Smooth Jazz version of Enter Sandman that is out on the net showing Metallica shredding some smooth jazz....',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-us) AppleWebKit/533.19.4 (KHTML, like Gecko)',	'',	212,	0),
(214,	1016,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-15 17:24:38',	'2010-12-16 01:24:38',	'Ummm...guys, this IS totally a joke. It\'s edited video of a Kenny G gig with the original audio replaced by a Coltrane album.\r\n\r\n@Bret, that video is pretty impressive. Kenny is definitely in a class of his own when it comes to the smooth jazz style of playing.  \r\n\r\nSorry if I disappointed everyone about it really being Kenny on the video! :)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.215 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	1),
(222,	756,	'Sam Sadigursky',	'sadigursky@gmail.com',	'http://www.SamSadigursky.com',	'207.38.131.12',	'2010-12-17 14:14:17',	'2010-12-17 22:14:17',	'I\'m not quite sure that a darker sound like that of Lee Konitz is due to a lack of overtones necessarily. There are a lot of other factors that go into a more cutting, Sanborn-like sound, baffle and reed strength being at the top of the list. I am sure that a lot of players with \"darker\" sounds have practiced overtones extensively, people like Joe Lovano, Mark Turner, Bill McHenry, and Billy Drewes. Steve Lacy was well known for his overtone practice, and he had what many people would term a dark sound. \r\n\r\nI think overtones are great for developing any kind of sound that a player is going for, and also for developing good note placement and pitch, which is closely related to positioning your larynx properly, or what many call \"voicing\". And they don\'t have to be boring to work on...I always encourage students to come up with their own ways of practicing overtones to keep things interesting. This way it\'s easier to make them part of your regular practice routine.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.5; en-US; rv:1.9.2.8) Gecko/20100722 Firefox/3.6.8',	'',	0,	0),
(223,	756,	'Doron',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-17 14:33:10',	'2010-12-17 22:33:10',	'Heya Sam! Well, the way I see it is that the equipment can assist you in bringing out those upper partials, but the bottom line is that those upper partials are more evident in the sound of someone like David Sanborn as compared to Lee Konitz. It\'s just like on an EQ, there\'s a very big difference when you roll off the highs, and although the overtones are present in Konitz\'s sound, he\'s using his embouchure to \"roll them off\" a bit in order to get his cool and piping sound.  \r\n\r\nI actually read up on overtones in \"The Master Speaks\" by one of Allard\'s students, and Joe taught that practicing overtones will allow you to play with a dark tone, but still have the upper overtones *when you need them*, so that implies that there are situations where it\'s possible to not utilize the full harmonic spectrum when you\'re going for a certain kind of sound. \r\n\r\nTaking that into consideration, I would imagine that any great sax player would practice overtones quite a bit regardless of the sound they were going for, as overtones allow you to master the crucial subtleties of one\'s embouchure. \r\n\r\nWe may be on the same page with this and just talking semantics, so sorry if I ramble a bit. :)\r\n\r\nThanks Sam!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.231 Safari/534.10',	'',	222,	1),
(235,	922,	'Rick Hirsch',	'rick@hirschmusic.com',	'http://www.hirschmusic.com',	'68.232.115.161',	'2010-12-21 11:14:07',	'2010-12-21 17:14:07',	'Doron,\r\n\r\nI always think of working on overtones primarily as something we do to help develop our vocal / voicing mechanism.  \r\n\r\nIt is my understanding that our tonal quality and timbre on the horn is significantly shaped and influenced by this vocal/voicing mechanism.  Without this development, a saxophonist is basically prepubescent, tone-wise.\r\n\r\nfurther thoughts?\r\n\r\n~ Rick',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_5_8; en-us) AppleWebKit/533.19.4 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.0.3 Safari/533.19.4',	'',	0,	0),
(238,	922,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-21 15:40:34',	'2010-12-21 23:40:34',	'Yeah, totally, that\'s why I mention precisely that point in the \"Embouchure\" section of the article, so I totally agree with you. \r\n\r\nI suppose I didn\'t necessarily organize the information to cite overtone practice as primarily a voicing exercise since there are other benefits to it, but I do agree that the ability to consciously shape ones tone is among the biggest, if not THE biggest benefit of these exercises.\r\n\r\nSometimes though, overtones are looked at as a way to practice altissimo and nothing else. Or we just struggle to get all of the partials out and just leave it at that. So it\'s good to keep focused on the real goal at hand, which is to use the embouchure and the larynx to form the sound you want to achieve. \r\n\r\nDoes that make sense?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.231 Safari/534.10',	'',	235,	1),
(239,	756,	'Are You a Mindful Saxophone Player? | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//are-you-mindful-saxophone-player/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2010-12-22 08:04:46',	'2010-12-22 16:04:46',	'[...] only on the steadiness of harmonic color in my sound. In other words, I focus on hearing the same overtones at both pianissimo and [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.0.3',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(240,	272,	'Back on the Sax Part 1: Longtones | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//back-on-the-sax-part-1-longtones/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2010-12-22 08:17:48',	'2010-12-22 16:17:48',	'[...] other day, my good friend and amazing saxophonist Ricky Sweum reminded me that since they&#8217;re so far removed from the things that students need to do, [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.0.3',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(254,	1112,	'Neal',	'jammin@saxstation.com',	'https://SaxStation.com',	'207.177.237.4',	'2010-12-28 22:33:32',	'2010-12-29 06:33:32',	'Hey Doron,\r\nThat\'s a good point.  You hear from a young age that you should transcribe.  It does a lot for your skills on sax.\r\n\r\nStarting with bop solos doesn\'t seem to be the right process though.  \r\n\r\nMelodies \'make more sense\' and have more patterns generally.\r\n\r\n-Neal',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; en-US; rv:1.9.2.13) Gecko/20101203 Firefox/3.6.13 GTB7.1',	'',	0,	0),
(255,	690,	'Jack',	'f_minor@hotmail.co.uk',	'',	'82.13.152.54',	'2010-12-29 04:19:14',	'2010-12-29 12:19:14',	'Glad the Lucas Pickford site could help. still need to check out the rest of these sites, especially Cannonball. :)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 6.0; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.224 Safari/534.10',	'',	0,	0),
(256,	690,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-29 10:40:40',	'2010-12-29 18:40:40',	'Yeah, there\'s some great resources here, definitely check out as many as you can! \r\n\r\nSo did you have anything to do with the Lucas Pickford site?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.231 Safari/534.10',	'',	255,	1),
(259,	1083,	'Back on the Sax Part 2: Palm Keys | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//back-on-the-sax-part-2-palm-keys/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2010-12-30 08:03:50',	'2010-12-30 16:03:50',	'[...] So here I am with the second installment of Back on the Sax where I detail my adventures as I go from tech and blogging geek back into my former status of professional giggin&#8217; sax player. To read more about the thinking behind this series you can check out my first Back on the Sax post. [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.0.3',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(261,	270,	'Ross Walters',	'ross@rosswalters.com',	'',	'97.114.118.166',	'2010-12-30 10:11:03',	'2010-12-30 16:11:03',	'The harmonica is a popular double for myself and many sax players I know. Being a strong harmonica player opened the door to one of the most important gigs in my life. Pennywhistle is another handy little double that can add a celtic sound to your repitoure. I think every gigging sax player should learn to shake a tamborine and a shaker. Percussion, guitar, keys, vocals, these are all super valuable, non-traditional \'rock and roll doubles\' that can open doors into higher paying gigs. You can drive yourself mad trying to get yourself on some kind of marathon practice routine on all these instruments. That doesn\'t work. You go through seasons where you are passionate about one of them and you make a great deal of progress on it and then, as the muse strikes, you move on to something else.\r\nI admire players that can stick to one instrument and be content. I just hear more sounds in my head than what the tenor sax can produce.\r\nSomeone asked tenor player Pete Christlieb at a club performance if he brought his clarinet \"It\'s in my closet squeaking\" he said. Which is a funny line.\r\nThe clarinet has fallen out of favor with sax players but it sure was a neat sound back in the day when arrangers would have the whole sax section switch to clarinet for part of a tune.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.4; en-US; rv:1.9.1.5) Gecko/20091102 Firefox/3.5.5',	'',	0,	0),
(262,	270,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2010-12-30 10:14:49',	'2010-12-30 18:14:49',	'Wow, doubling on harmonica and pennywhistle, never thought of that but it makes total sense. It\'s really cool to read how the definition of \"doubling\" has shifted so much from the big band and studio definitions of the past into the many permutations we\'ve seen in the last 20 years or so. \r\n\r\nI do know that playing flute and clarinet can still increase one\'s income by quite a bit, but at the same time, doing what you love is really the most profitable thing in the long run. \r\n\r\nThanks for the insights Ross!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.231 Safari/534.10',	'',	261,	1),
(265,	1016,	'- Mark',	'mamadden@nyc.rr.com',	'',	'24.90.203.46',	'2010-12-31 19:02:54',	'2011-01-01 03:02:54',	'Well, this clearly wasn\'t Mr. Garelick.  Almost like trying to pass off Benny Golson as Wayne Shorter.  (private joke).',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 7.0; Windows NT 5.1; .NET CLR 1.1.4322; .NET CLR 2.0.50727; .NET CLR 3.0.04506.30; InfoPath.1; .NET CLR 3.0.04506.648; .NET CLR 3.0.4506.2152; .NET CLR 3.5.30729)',	'',	0,	0),
(266,	690,	'Jack',	'f_minor@hotmail.co.uk',	'',	'82.13.152.54',	'2011-01-01 05:53:06',	'2011-01-01 13:53:06',	'No I don\'t, It\'s just something I came across when writing out stuff for an ensemble actually, he\'s done a lot of stuff that\'s really useful.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 6.0; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.224 Safari/534.10',	'',	256,	0),
(267,	690,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2011-01-01 12:12:22',	'2011-01-01 20:12:22',	'Yeah, it\'s amazing that we have such generous people in the jazz community, and cool that you were able to use his stuff for your writing as well.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.231 Safari/534.10',	'',	266,	1),
(268,	1016,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2011-01-01 12:15:54',	'2011-01-01 20:15:54',	'Hmm, Benny Golson for Wayne Shorter, or Kenny G for Coltrane...I think that Kenny and Coltrane make for a bigger stretch... ;)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_5; en-US) AppleWebKit/534.10 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/8.0.552.231 Safari/534.10',	'',	265,	1),
