(831,	1797,	'12 Tips for Hitting that Altissimo G Like a Pro | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//12-tips-for-hitting-that-altissimo-g-like-a-pro/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2011-07-25 06:02:03',	'2011-07-25 13:02:03',	'[...] to delve into the altissimo register. While there is a great deal of information out there about the art of altissimo as a whole, I thought that it would be good to hone in on a specific note in the altissimo register that seems [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.2.1',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(832,	538,	'Phil',	'pabs@absamail.co.za',	'',	'196.30.245.149',	'2011-07-25 07:23:34',	'2011-07-25 14:23:34',	'1. Crack in time – Calvin Harris\r\n2. Sad Cafe – The Eagles\r\n3. All my Life – Phil Collins\r\n4. House on the Hill – Audience\r\n5. You aint smiling – Audience\r\n6. Urgent – Foreigner\r\n7. Bad to the bone – George Thorogood\r\n8. Celtic Swing – van Morrison\r\n9. Die Trying – Dave Matthews Band',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/533.20.25 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.0.4 Safari/533.20.27',	'',	0,	0),
(834,	538,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-25 12:39:47',	'2011-07-25 19:39:47',	'Hello Phil,\r\n\r\nWow, truth be told, many of these are unfamiliar, but the Urgent solo is a great example of rock and roll sax for sure. \r\n\r\nThanks so much for your list - keep on rockin\'!\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.30 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/12.0.742.122 Safari/534.30',	'',	832,	1),
(835,	538,	'Phil',	'pabs@absamail.co.za',	'',	'196.30.245.149',	'2011-07-26 00:35:34',	'2011-07-26 07:35:34',	'You are welcome. I made a SAX TRAX compilation CD\r\nfor myself, of the 16 best sax recordings I could find.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US) AppleWebKit/533.20.25 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.0.4 Safari/533.20.27',	'',	834,	0),
(836,	538,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-26 08:03:49',	'2011-07-26 15:03:49',	'Wow, sounds cool. Must be quite a journey to listen to all of those in a row. :-)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.30 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/12.0.742.122 Safari/534.30',	'',	835,	1),
(837,	805,	'Tim Willcox',	'timothywillcox@gmail.com',	'',	'24.20.143.240',	'2011-07-26 11:01:14',	'2011-07-26 17:01:14',	'Hi Doron, \r\n\r\nI finally got around to listening to this all the way through. Thanks for putting up with my inability to complete a full sentence, not to mention all of the awkward pauses! \r\n\r\nThanks for having me on your site. I appreciate what you\'re doing for players and teachers. \r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nTim',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:5.0.1) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/5.0.1',	'',	0,	0),
(838,	2417,	'Bill Plake',	'bplake@earthlink.net',	'https://billplakemusic.org',	'99.109.197.253',	'2011-07-26 17:34:23',	'2011-07-27 00:34:23',	'This is a great post. Fantastic advice for not only the beginning improviser, but also for the seasoned, serious artist. Randy describes etude writing as \"an excellent practice method for learning to assemble your musical ideas.\" I couldn\'t agree more. This was standard practice for all the \"Tristano school\" improvisers, and is something that I insist upon with all my improvisation students. \r\n\r\nIt\'s important to remember (I think) that improvisation is spontaneous composition. Therefore, all great improvisation is also great composition. Writing etudes helps the improviser crystalize his or her ideas into meaningful, personal music. Another gem of an interview. Thanks!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	0,	0),
(839,	805,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-26 21:13:30',	'2011-07-27 04:13:30',	'Heya Tim,\r\n\r\nNo problem man. Hell, I can barely stand to listen to myself with all of the \"umm\"s and \"very cool\"\'s. I think that the goal of the interview was definitely achieved, since it\'s packed with some great info. In fact, I now practice long tones with the fade in-fadeout method you introduced.\r\n\r\nAnyhow, thanks for the thanks, totally stoked that people are feeling helped by the site. \r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.30 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/12.0.742.122 Safari/534.30',	'',	837,	1),
(840,	2417,	'Randy Hunter',	'randy@randyhunterjazz.com',	'http://www.randyhunterjazz.com',	'76.105.114.143',	'2011-07-27 13:30:31',	'2011-07-27 19:30:31',	'Thanks for the great feedback, Bill. I started writing etude books about 10 years ago, and quickly recognized a substantial impact on my ability to organize and perform my ideas in solos. It was probably one of the most significant steps I had taken in the practice room in many years.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X 10_4_11; en) AppleWebKit/525.18 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/3.1.2 Safari/525.22',	'',	838,	0),
(844,	2256,	'Mastering Challenging Intervals Part II – Whole Steps | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'',	'69.89.25.172',	'2011-08-01 06:03:03',	'2011-08-01 12:03:03',	'[...] our first installment of this series, we established the importance of practicing transitioning between notes that are [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.2.1',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(862,	1382,	'Jason the Obscure',	'BFlat.harmonic@gmail.com',	'',	'99.34.78.110',	'2011-08-04 13:23:13',	'2011-08-04 20:23:13',	'Once again, this poll proves that reeds are much like horns and mouthpieces: what one reed/piece/horn/lig does for this person will not necessarily be what it does for that person. That doesn\'t make it bad/good except what works for you.\r\n\r\nI\'ve many boxes of Rico Royals, and find I agree with Doron on this one. Royals are just okay FOR ME. I\'m happy to  make a deal to those who like these.  Eight boxes of factory sealed Royals #3. I\'ve a lot of respect for Rico, they just don\'t work FOR ME.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; WOW64) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(863,	1278,	'Jason the Obscure',	'BFlat.harmonic@gmail.com',	'',	'99.34.78.110',	'2011-08-04 13:36:56',	'2011-08-04 20:36:56',	'Cheer for Vandoren Java reeds. Like everyone here, I\'ve tried many different reed producers from Marca, Brancher, and others, to Vandoren, Rico, Alexander and others.\r\n\r\nVandoren\'s Java reeds have been the ones FOR ME to return to for their sound quality, a clarity and balanced voice, their amazing responsiveness, they speak quickly, and their consistency. Frankly, after a very large number of boxes across both #2s and #3s, are there some better than others? Yes. I cannot, however, recall ever having a \"bad\" reed. That\'s amazing \r\n\r\nSome people feel as strongly about say, Rico. For you, if you have factory sealed Java\'s and want to trade for factory sealed Royals. Let me know.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; WOW64) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(864,	1398,	'Jason the Obscure',	'BFlat.harmonic@gmail.com',	'',	'99.34.78.110',	'2011-08-04 13:42:53',	'2011-08-04 20:42:53',	'V16s showed up for me like a good looking bloke at the bar who just wants to arm wrestle. Exhausting, stuffy reeds.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; WOW64) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(865,	1394,	'Jason the Obscure',	'BFlat.harmonic@gmail.com',	'',	'99.34.78.110',	'2011-08-04 13:49:59',	'2011-08-04 20:49:59',	'ZZs are better for me than V16s, but are less responsive than my Javas. If I had to choose between the V16 and ZZ, it would be ZZ for sure. Luckily for me, Vandoren makes Javas, so I\'m sticking with them for my tenor.\r\n\r\nI\'ve six boxes of factory sealed ZZ #3 and three boxes of ZZ #2.5 lemme know if interested.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; WOW64) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(869,	272,	'ben andre',	'BAndre@gmail.com',	'',	'24.168.9.80',	'2011-08-04 23:35:33',	'2011-08-05 06:35:33',	'great interview',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; rv:5.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/5.0',	'',	0,	0),
(870,	272,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-05 08:56:56',	'2011-08-05 15:56:56',	'Hello Ben,\r\n\r\nGlad you liked it! It was actually the first podcast I did and the sound quality is atrocious, which was too bad for Ricky. Fortunately his playing and expertise speak for themselves. :)\r\n\r\nThanks for the kind words,\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	869,	1),
(871,	2284,	'Ken',	'kfrank1@mindspring.com',	'',	'67.101.5.87',	'2011-08-06 15:37:06',	'2011-08-06 22:37:06',	'Liked this article. My teacher had me do similar things with the book like playing excerpts from a couple of studies in all keys, playing one study in F instead of C, then eventually taking through other keys. I also practise these on clarinet and flute as warmups.\r\n\r\nI sometimes wish I could just use one technique book for all my doubles since I have limited time. Maybe Klose could be good for this, although I don\'t know of anyone who does this.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.9.2.18) Gecko/20110614 Firefox/3.6.18 ( .NET CLR 3.5.30729; .NET4.0E)',	'',	0,	0),
(872,	2135,	'Ken',	'kfrank1@mindspring.com',	'',	'67.101.5.87',	'2011-08-06 15:59:16',	'2011-08-06 22:59:16',	'Interesting. I currently use Klose, Viola Technique of the Saxophone, and Universal Method for sax, Langenus Method for clarinet, Oppermann Velocity Studies for bass clarinet, and Wye Daily Studies for flute. I\'ve often wondered about whether my technique would improve or suffer if I say took one exercise at a time out of one of these books and practiced it on all instruments and practiced like that.\r\n\r\nHowever I don\'t know of anyone who does this. People seem to use sax books for sax, clarinet for clarinet, and flute for flute.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.9.2.18) Gecko/20110614 Firefox/3.6.18 ( .NET CLR 3.5.30729; .NET4.0E)',	'',	0,	0),
(873,	2135,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-06 17:34:20',	'2011-08-07 00:34:20',	'Hello Ken,\r\n\r\nI can\'t imagine how using any one of these books could degrade your technique on any instrument. Sounds like you hav a great regimen going with all of your books!\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	872,	1),
(880,	1596,	'A Look at the World&#8217;s Most Popular Saxophone Embouchure | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//a-look-at-the-worlds-most-popular-saxophone-embouchure/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2011-08-08 06:01:49',	'2011-08-08 13:01:49',	'[...] Espoused by greats such as George Garzone and Jerry Bergonzi is the &#8220;no embouchure embouchure&#8221; where the lip is positioned naturally, not necessarily in or out. But that&#8217;s a topic for a whole other article which you can read here. [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.2.1',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(882,	694,	'Edward Tisdale',	'edward_t@knac.com',	'',	'72.197.78.109',	'2011-08-09 14:34:05',	'2011-08-09 21:34:05',	'In school we always just used Johnson and Johnson tape',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:7.0a2) Gecko/20110805 Firefox/7.0a2',	'',	0,	0),
(883,	694,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-09 14:43:06',	'2011-08-09 21:43:06',	'That\'s some ninja action that is! :-)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.107 Safari/535.1',	'',	882,	1),
(889,	1593,	'Brad Carman',	'carman.music@gmail.com',	'https://bradcarmanmusic.com',	'184.60.15.186',	'2011-08-10 21:31:48',	'2011-08-11 04:31:48',	'I\'m with Doron.  I was lucky enough to hear Jon play in Madison, WI several months ago with a good friend of mine, Anders Svanoe (also a sax player).  They played a duo set of free jazz and killed it.  Jon has an amazing sound, great ideas, and is an all around nice guy.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 6.0; en-US; rv:1.9.2.18) Gecko/20110614 Firefox/3.6.18 ( .NET CLR 3.5.30729; .NET4.0C)',	'',	468,	0),
(893,	2385,	'John Bayne',	'johnbayne@ihug.co.nz',	'',	'118.92.98.77',	'2011-08-15 01:30:33',	'2011-08-15 07:30:33',	'I would like to know more about traditional Vandoren reeds because the Javas and V16 are no way as good as a good Rico Royal, #3, , #2.5 or #2. However, for clarinet, for me there is nothing like a Vandoren Ru Lepic #2.5 for everyting including consistency. So I have a healthy respect for vandoren their reeds always look better made but so far, in spite of Rico\'s inconsistency, their reeds have more vibrancy, richer harnonics and more springiness. But I may never have tried a traditional Vandoren so would appreciate knowing more, so I can find some and try them, as finding good reeds is the real headache for me about saxophones.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 7.0; Windows NT 6.0; EasyBits GO v1.0; SLCC1; .NET CLR 2.0.50727; Media Center PC 5.0; .NET CLR 3.0.04506; InfoPath.2)',	'',	0,	0),
(894,	1394,	'John Bayne',	'johnbayne@ihug.co.nz',	'',	'118.92.98.77',	'2011-08-15 01:53:19',	'2011-08-15 07:53:19',	'Exactly my own reason for choosing Rico Royal, in that I put consistency behind performance. I am getting less still out of a box of ten, but still have stuck to Rico Roayl since around the late 1960s I think. I would like to comment on your choice of mouthpiece. From around 1971 when I discovered Otto Link Supertone (bell metal) Florida tenor saxophone mouthpieces, they were the only mouthpieces I liked after having bought a number of Berg Larsens, Geoff Lawton, Guy Hawkins, Brilhart Level Air and Dukoff to name some of them. I also only liked metal mouthpieces until I came across one of the rarest mouthpieces today and that is a genuine one tip opening offer vintage J&amp;D Hite rod rubber mouthpiece. I have owned in Florida Supertone bell metal links, all star models unfortunately, 5,7,8,9,10 and a special 11* which owner Ben Harrod made for me early 1974 to cope with amplified bands when I may not be amplified and it worked brilliantly. Until recently,it was only on this mouthpiece that I could get above the four octave limit to just over five octaves. However, David Hite\'s mouthpiece design I liked even better again, and so I phoned him up in 1974 and begged him to make me an exact replica in solid sterling silver, believing that the thicker the walls of both mouthpiece, neck, body, bow and bell the better and the more smoother, creamier and three dimensional the tone. David Hite being a very busy man and not liking metal mouthpieces (I only knew this separately and never mentioned it of course) was neither equipped nor inspired to do this for me, but being a very nice and generous man devoted to the cause unselfishly, he suggested that if I wanted it so badly why not make it myself, and so I made what could be the world\'s first 1.spun cast 2. solid stering silver 3. rod rubber designed 4. open chambered roll over baffle mouthpiece and it is of course my favourite mouthpiece. There are still some features about the original in rod rubber which I prefer, like when I compared a reall good MKVI tenor sax, twice about five years apart, and it did have a more user friendly way about it, but I could not bear to part with my larger than life Silversonic s/n 719736 which being from new, I had the body and bow silver plated to match the neck and bell with a wash of gold inside the bell, and with my rod rubber designed solid spun cast sterling silver mouthpiece it looked really neat all bright silver.What I am getting at about the New York Link is that both the Florida Links and the Hites were my favourite mouthpieces to the extent that I did not like playing anyting else of what I had seen so far, and yet Babbitts took over both and utterly ruined them for me. I have recently bought but not received yet a James Houlik which looks surprisingly like a vintage Hite, and if I had time back I would buy a Bilger if I could as these both seem to have extra fat bodies, the chamber the same size as the rest and a roll over baffle. If you could get a vintage Florida Otto Link especially a #6 which I borrowed once, it was much better than the 6*. Today I like the tip opening range from 0.105 to 0.120\" with around 0.110\" the most likely favourite opening and a lenght of facing of probably that of the #8 Otto Link which is 0.968\" in a special Link three curve facing according to what Ben Harrod told me.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 7.0; Windows NT 6.0; EasyBits GO v1.0; SLCC1; .NET CLR 2.0.50727; Media Center PC 5.0; .NET CLR 3.0.04506; InfoPath.2)',	'',	0,	0),
(895,	1394,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-15 09:10:57',	'2011-08-15 16:10:57',	'Wow, thanks for such a detailed reviews and analysis!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	894,	1),
(896,	2553,	'ericdano',	'ericdano@gmail.com',	'',	'99.9.96.175',	'2011-08-15 12:53:57',	'2011-08-15 19:53:57',	'That is it? Those are the \"Killer\" practice aids?\r\n\r\n#2 is lame. I\'d get Band in a Box. The base version is $129 and you get everything #2 and so much more. Plus you can record yourself, which is a killer aid to practicing and you strangely omitted. I\'d say it is a MUST for any student of jazz or pro wanting to work out new progressions.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	0,	0),
(897,	2553,	'Bill Plake',	'bplake@earthlink.net',	'https://billplakemusic.org',	'99.3.80.84',	'2011-08-15 20:51:22',	'2011-08-16 03:51:22',	'The tuning CD is an immensely valuable thing. Far too many saxophonists (and other musicians) try to improve their intonation primarily by playing with a tuner, then (I guess) \"memorize\" the feeling of playing in tune. Helpful as this might be, I don\'t think it is nearly as practical as learning to really hear and respond to pitch changes, like you\'re going to have to do in the real world.\r\n\r\nAs for the Paul Carman CD metronome, I think it too is a good idea. I\'ve been playing jazz and other improvised music for over 35 years, and a lot of the practice I\'m doing now, I actually don\'t want to hear the harmonic form being played behind me. Rather, I want to be able to internalize the sound of the form in my inner ear and mind. This gives me great freedom and helps my imagination and ears immensely. \r\n\r\nI have Band in a Box, too, and think it\'s a great thing. One is not better than the other in my opinion, they just have different possibilities, purposes and price points. When I teach a beginning jazz student, I give him or her lots of Band in a Box tracks to work with. But as they improve, I want them to work more and more with responding directly, openly and specifically with just the time feel. The Metronome CD is perfect for that, and it has the convenience of being ready to go (and for very little money).\r\n\r\nAnyhow, I really appreciated the post, and like the fact that there\'s somebody else out there who values the same products I do.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	0,	0),
(901,	2553,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-16 18:54:04',	'2011-08-17 01:54:04',	'Hello Eric,\r\n\r\nI appreciate what you\'re saying, and I\'m sorry if the title misled you to think that this was a longer list. \r\n\r\nAs for #2, I\'d say that this is different from Band in a Box in that if you were playing through song forms, you\'d have the advantage of the chords playing behind you and helping you to keep the tune\'s form, so this CD is definitely a bit more challenging. I also think it focuses the ear more on time feel than playing with a full rhythm section.\r\n\r\nAnyhow, thanks for chiming in, and I hope that you come back as I\'ve got some cool articles coming down the pike that I think you\'ll enjoy.\r\n\r\nBest,\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	896,	1),
(902,	2553,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-16 18:58:08',	'2011-08-17 01:58:08',	'Hello Bill,\r\n\r\nGlad you liked the article. :-)\r\n\r\nAs for the tuning, I agree, it\'s good to practice with moving pitches. I actually did a video article about this very topic which you can checkout here if you like: https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//do-you-play-out-of-tune/\r\n\r\nThanks for stopping by,\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	897,	1),
(903,	2553,	'ericdano',	'ericdano@gmail.com',	'',	'99.9.96.175',	'2011-08-16 19:12:36',	'2011-08-17 02:12:36',	'Yeah, title was a tad misleading.......\r\n\r\nBand in a Box would be more flexible in that you could mute various instruments, so you could do just drums, or bass, or drums and bass, or just piano, or drums and piano.....or whatever. \r\n\r\nI mean, of the TWO things listed, band in a box would do what they do and then some......',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	901,	0),
(904,	2553,	'ericdano',	'ericdano@gmail.com',	'',	'99.9.96.175',	'2011-08-16 19:15:54',	'2011-08-17 02:15:54',	'I\'d still say that band in a box is better. For instance, the Metronome CD, say you just want to give your student a Afro-Cuban beat to work with......or a Gypsy Jazz beat.\r\n\r\nSo, it does the same thing except better. Way better (assuming you have the version with Real Tracks). \r\n\r\nI\'d say it is essential for anyone learning jazz.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	897,	0),
(905,	2553,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-16 19:16:59',	'2011-08-17 02:16:59',	'Well, the time feel on Band in a Box would not match that of a real drummer, thereby making it a bit of a less realistic playing situation. \r\n\r\nThat said, I hear what you\'re saying about Band in a Box, so if that\'s works best for you, then go for it! These are just 2 suggestions of tools that, as far as I know, aren\'t immediately obvious to most musicians.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	903,	1),
(906,	2553,	'ericdano',	'ericdano@gmail.com',	'',	'99.9.96.175',	'2011-08-16 19:22:05',	'2011-08-17 02:22:05',	'Um, no. You obviously haven\'t heard the RealTracks. It has excellent feel.....and the guitar tracks are awesome. Oh, and they have Eric Marienthal doing some of the sax tracks....and those too are generally good (depends on the chord progressions, sometimes the solos don\'t have good flow to them)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	905,	0),
(910,	2564,	'Marc Plotkin',	'marc@marcplotkin.com',	'https://www.marcplotkin.com',	'96.242.123.240',	'2011-08-18 15:58:36',	'2011-08-18 22:58:36',	'Very interest post. My only beef is I think the section about discouraging young players from teaching even younger players is a bit of an old-school mentality. Some of the more innovative schools have begun making final exams for students being based on how well a concept can be explained to another student. There are a multitude of scientific studies (just Google \"Learning by Teaching\") supporting that this practice cements concepts in students who act as teachers much more than not.\r\n\r\nI myself taught elementary school private lessons in my town back when I was in high school and it was one of the most enlightening experiences I ever had as a musician. It built up my complete understanding of whatever concept was being explained, greatly improved my communication skills, and started me on a path to correlating the musical knowledge I\'ve built with compensation. Even though I didn\'t have a music degree yet, I had been taking private lessons with a pro for years and essentially passed on what had been taught to me to youngsters.\r\n\r\nI also think building an association of tangible value (accepting $ from Sally\'s Mom) with the knowledge the student is imparting on the younger student would be a positive thing. We live in a world where most musicians are content providing their art and their knowledge to others for free (myself occasionally included). We might live in a world where musicians are respected more if at an early stage it\'s not assumed that this is a work that goes unnoticed monetarily. I\'m in no way suggesting emphasizing money over the heart and joy of just playing music, but starting to plant a seed that thinking about money in regards to teaching and playing music at a young stage seems to me to lead to a more balanced outlook and outcome.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(912,	2564,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-18 21:57:06',	'2011-08-19 04:57:06',	'I have to say that I actually agree with you Mark. I also taught in high school - but I hated doing it so naturally I wasn\'t exactly the world\'s greatest teacher. \r\n\r\nBut I\'m sure that there are some high school players out there who give college players and maybe even some pros a good run for their money, so I don\'t think you can make a blanket statement that high school students should not be teaching, period (and I highly doubt that David was trying to say that, I think he was just thinking in terms of not screwing up younger students by passing on one\'s own undeveloped playing habits).   \r\n\r\nAnyhow, thanks for chiming in my friend!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	910,	1),
(917,	2553,	'John Nastos',	'john@johnnastos.com',	'https://johnnastos.com',	'50.53.212.225',	'2011-08-21 10:26:19',	'2011-08-21 17:26:19',	'Playing with a CD like that can be great, as can playing with Band in a Box, as they both give you real grooves to practice with that don\'t rush or drag.\r\n\r\nI found that for my playing, though, I was relying on those types of tools, or typical metronomes, as a crutch, allowing them to remind me where the time is.\r\n\r\nI created a metronome for iOS to practice with that doesn\'t let me get lazy with the time -- Metronomics (metronomicsapp.com).  There are a bunch of settings available for having it only play the subdivisions some of the time, leaving bars out, etc, making you keep more of the time going yourself.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_7) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(919,	2553,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-21 13:33:56',	'2011-08-21 20:33:56',	'Yeah, it looks like an awesome tool. Too bad I don\'t have a frickin\' iPhone!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.112 Safari/535.1',	'',	917,	1),
(921,	2576,	'Sam Sadigursky',	'sadigursky@gmail.com',	'http://www.SamSadigursky.com',	'207.38.131.12',	'2011-08-22 16:10:31',	'2011-08-22 23:10:31',	'Matt is hands down one of the finest repairman out there, and definitely somebody of rare integrity. Thanks for sharing these insights. There\'s definitely more than meets the eye to these horns, and sending a student out to buy a new horn is not so simple these days. Everybody should really do their research, and make sure somebody that plays gives anything a try before buying it!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.5; en-US; rv:1.9.2.8) Gecko/20100722 Firefox/3.6.8',	'',	0,	0),
(922,	2484,	'Sam Sadigursky',	'sadigursky@gmail.com',	'http://www.SamSadigursky.com',	'207.38.131.12',	'2011-08-22 16:13:36',	'2011-08-22 23:13:36',	'\"Tenacity, Determination.\"\r\n\r\nWell stated, Jay.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.5; en-US; rv:1.9.2.8) Gecko/20100722 Firefox/3.6.8',	'',	0,	0),
(923,	2576,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-22 21:05:13',	'2011-08-23 04:05:13',	'Whoa, cool that you know Matt! Although I haven\'t had the pleasure of having him work on one of my horns, I can definitely agree about the integrity and in depth knowledge he has. Glad you dug the article. :-)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	921,	1),
(926,	2629,	'Rick',	'rick@hirschmusic.com',	'http://www.hirschmusic.com',	'68.232.115.161',	'2011-08-23 09:07:08',	'2011-08-23 15:07:08',	'I also use RSJ on alto (2H filed &amp; unfiled) &amp; tenor (3S unfiled).  They seem to have been working well for me for several years.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X 10.5; en-US; rv:1.9.2.8) Gecko/20100722 Firefox/3.6.8',	'',	0,	0),
(927,	1299,	'Austin Alianiello',	'teetime661@gmail.com',	'',	'67.86.2.132',	'2011-08-23 20:02:49',	'2011-08-24 03:02:49',	'I have really enjoyed playing on these reeds. One thing that factored into my selection of them is how versatile they are. Jazz ensembles, solo work, concert band, La Voz really work well for anything. In my opinion, these reeds sound unlike any other, much more open and clear. They are very free blowing and project well on my Otto Link STM, but can easily blend with an ensemble when playing on my hard rubber mouthpiece. For me they haven\'t been the most consistent in strength, but pretty much every reed is playable. They seem like an ideal reed for someone who likes to work on reeds even though I do not, and for $24 for a box of ten reeds, the same price as 5 Vandorens, it seems like a good choice. I like the sound I get on them more than that of the Rico Select Jazz and Vandoren ZZ, but I think that the best reeds I have ever tried are the Roberto\'s Winds reeds from NYC, which are actually made by Rigotti. However, $50 a box seems absurd. I would like to pick up a box of Javas because I have heard great things, but they are double the cost of the La Voz and may not be too buzzy for concert band. To me, it seems that the La Voz get better the more I play them. What seems like a so-so reed at the beginning a gig will usually be giving me a great sound by the end. It\'s also very reassuring to know that these reeds have been used by some of my favorite players like Michael Brecker, Joe Henderson, Sonny Rollins, and including who I believe to be the sax player with the greatest sound ever, Dexter Gordon. As a side note I preferred the look of the old boxes more than the ones today. All things considered, I still think that these are the best reeds for me at this point in my development, but I would not hesitate to try others. The best reeds I have ever played on have come out of a box of La Voz reeds!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_7) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	0,	0),
(928,	1299,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-23 21:52:46',	'2011-08-24 04:52:46',	'Wow Austin, thanks so much for the in-depth review! You bring up a great point about getting the bang for your buck with these vs. Vandoren\'s (although I can\'t see myself straying from my beloved Vandoren Java\'s and ZZ\'s).',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	927,	1),
(932,	2645,	'Bill Plake',	'bplake@earthlink.net',	'https://billplakemusic.org',	'99.3.80.84',	'2011-08-25 16:52:45',	'2011-08-25 23:52:45',	'Mel Martin is a complete musician: great saxophonist, improviser, composer and band leader. HIs performing credits attest to his high degree of skill. He\'s also a true gentleman, very generous with his knowledge and experience. I particularly like how he described his current practice as \"noodling\". When you\'ve worked as long and as mindfully as he has, I think that is exactly the type of exploratory practice that is warranted. As he said, \"lots of \'go for it\' time. Again, thanks for a nice interview.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	0,	0),
(934,	2645,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-25 21:12:05',	'2011-08-26 04:12:05',	'I completely agree with everything you\'re saying. And btw, I just took a look at your site, and wow, you\'ve got some amazing stuff there. I\'ll definitely let my Twitter and Facebook friends know about it, but I think that there\'s a lot of people out there that would glean so much from your blog. Let me know if there\'s anything I can do to help you.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	932,	1),
(937,	1299,	'Austin Alianiello',	'teetime661@gmail.com',	'',	'67.86.2.132',	'2011-08-26 19:33:58',	'2011-08-27 02:33:58',	'Just curious Doron, what dictates whether you play on the ZZ or Java? Different reeds for different horns, for different mouthpieces, or different playing situations? Or is it just a matter of what is available?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_7) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	928,	0),
(941,	1299,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-27 10:49:05',	'2011-08-27 17:49:05',	'Hello Austin,\r\n\r\nThese days I only play jazz and I don\'t find myself needing to sound different depending on the musical situation. Now if I were a studio musician, then it would almost certainly be a different situation where I might very well have different reeds, mouthpieces, and possibly even horns based on the musical setting. . \r\n\r\nTo me it\'s a simple matter of personal taste as to what sounds better to me. So I don\'t really favor one reed or another based on what horn or mouthpiece I\'m playing. I just pick whichever sounds and feels best given my current horn and mouthpiece combination. \r\n\r\nLet me know if that doesn\'t answer your question. \r\n\r\nThanks for reaching out!\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	937,	1),
(945,	2576,	'Is it Worth Buying a &#8220;Pro&#8221; Model Saxophone? &#8211; Part II | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//is-it-worth-buying-a-pro-model-saxophone-part-ii/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2011-08-29 06:03:26',	'2011-08-29 13:03:26',	'[...] If you haven&#8217;t done so already, check out part I to get the full scoop. [...]',	0,	'1',	'The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/3.2.1',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(946,	2589,	'Ericdano',	'Ericdano@jazz-sax.com',	'',	'99.9.96.175',	'2011-08-29 09:14:28',	'2011-08-29 16:14:28',	'I generally agree with all his comments. Except the Yamaha stuff. I love the 23s. Great student horns. But for $40 more (via wwbw.com) they have the intermediate 475 models which seem dull. I\'ve had a number of students who have rented these from a local store, and they seem to fall apart way more than the 23s. And then we get back to the pricing thing again. Why have your student and intermediate models so close in price and then have the pro horns a lot higher? I think Yamaha would be wiser to get the 23 line down into the lower $1000 range rather than having them be right next to the intermediate line in price. Or just kill off one of the lines.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPad; U; CPU OS 4_3_3 like Mac OS X; en-us) AppleWebKit/533.17.9 (KHTML, like Gecko) Mobile/8J3',	'',	0,	0),
(947,	2589,	'matt stohrer',	'stohrermusic@gmail.com',	'https://stohrermusic.com',	'174.109.90.186',	'2011-08-29 10:13:38',	'2011-08-29 17:13:38',	'Eric,\r\n\r\nThanks for reading!\r\n\r\nI wouldn\'t be surprised if they did, or if the 475 replaced the 23 eventually or if they melded into a new model.  Definitely a weird situation to have them priced so close.  The gap used to be a bit wider, but it continues to close.  \r\n\r\nInteresting fact: in some markets, the 475 model is called the 32 model.  \r\n\r\nThere is a definite difference in ergonomics between the two models- the 23 has rather simpler and more durable keywork design, while the 475 is basically a modern professional-style mechanism with many ergonomic improvements but (IMHO) not as durable.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; WOW64) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	946,	0),
(950,	1596,	'toni',	'tonizucchi@gmail.com',	'',	'79.7.237.246',	'2011-08-30 04:02:23',	'2011-08-30 11:02:23',	'this wonderful  piece opened my eyes to the world of blowing the saxophone since a half a century!\r\nMy god, i cant\'t believe that i could play so easy using the suggestions especially   after havin\' looked at the photos.\r\nThank you very much and please continue in the teaching!\r\nToni',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	0,	0),
(951,	1596,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-30 16:22:53',	'2011-08-30 23:22:53',	'Hello Toni,\r\n\r\nWow, I can\'t say how happy I am that this article helped you as much as it did! I think that looking closely at the embouchure is important for all of us to do so that we\'re not just blindly blowing out of the horn, but consciously creating the sound that\'s in our ears. \r\n\r\nThanks for the kind words, all the best,\r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	950,	1),
(952,	1398,	'william',	'kherpel900@gmail.com',	'',	'69.247.116.27',	'2011-08-30 19:21:55',	'2011-08-31 02:21:55',	'i play the A. sax in the Oak Grove Band. and ive got to say these reeds are amazing. i highly recomend these reeds. ',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPod; U; CPU iPhone OS 4_1 like Mac OS X; en-us) AppleWebKit/532.9 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/4.0.5 Mobile/8B117 Safari/6531.22.7',	'',	0,	0),
(953,	1398,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-30 20:32:50',	'2011-08-31 03:32:50',	'Thanks William, glad to hear you\'ve found a great reed!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	952,	1),
(955,	1398,	'Jason The Obscure',	'BFlat.harmonic@gmail.com',	'',	'99.34.78.110',	'2011-08-31 21:19:58',	'2011-09-01 04:19:58',	'Well there\'s proof from William\'s post, that different reeds do different things for different people. I don\'t know what it is but suspect the sum total of one\'s embouchure, mouth, throat, and playing dynamics make is useful for reed makers to keep on making different reeds.\r\n\r\nI\'ve many boxes of factory sealed Vandoren V16s and will make a good deal if you want them. They don\'t work for me. V16s are one of the most beautiful reeds I\'ve seen. Yet for playin, I prefer Vandoren Java.......go figure.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; MSIE 9.0; Windows NT 6.1; WOW64; Trident/5.0; MAAU)',	'',	0,	0),
(956,	1398,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-31 21:23:18',	'2011-09-01 04:23:18',	'Yep, it\'s incredibly subjective. I think it\'s cool that there are so many different brands out there for all of the millions of saxophone player physical  permutations. \r\n\r\nFunny thing is, in the end, the impact on your sound as perceived by others is so much less than I imagine.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/535.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/13.0.782.215 Safari/535.1',	'',	955,	1),
(959,	1763,	'Ray Beatty',	'sailing@hawaii.rr.com',	'',	'98.150.229.73',	'2011-09-01 11:56:41',	'2011-09-01 18:56:41',	'I caught Bob\'s  \" Close Your Eyes\"on the radio while driving and had to buy the album--great stuff.\r\nHe\'s has a  beautiful and interesting style.\r\nI was lucky to get a lesson from him when I was in LA 3 years ago.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_6_8) AppleWebKit/534.50 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/5.1 Safari/534.50',	'',	0,	0),
