(206931,	6151,	'Gary Mosse',	'10211462938509389@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/10211462938509389',	'',	'2018-01-03 11:43:51',	'2018-01-03 17:43:51',	'A sequence in music is repeating a motif up to 3x\'s with each motif up or down a similar interval.  arpggios, small parts of different modal scales can be included.  Playing only pre-planed sequences of intervals is not music &amp; is boring!',	0,	'1',	'SNAP',	'',	0,	0),
(206932,	6151,	'Pat Carmody',	'10215415418238416@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/10215415418238416',	'',	'2018-01-03 11:04:39',	'2018-01-03 17:04:39',	'HEY..you guys work FAST!   thanks a TON AND 3/4 !!!! pc',	0,	'1',	'SNAP',	'',	0,	0),
(206934,	1953,	'Galen Fast',	'10156026432920682@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/10156026432920682',	'',	'2018-01-10 23:27:57',	'2018-01-11 05:27:57',	'I was taught to play with a looser embouchure for jazz than classical. Same concept.',	0,	'1',	'SNAP',	'',	0,	0),
(206935,	1953,	'Gerald Jonutz',	'1990311674570921@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/1990311674570921',	'',	'2018-01-11 02:21:27',	'2018-01-11 08:21:27',	'Not buying this. Find your sound and pitch and don\'t purposely tune sharp to achieve a better sound. I thought the sound sounded better in the natural tuning position.',	0,	'1',	'SNAP',	'',	0,	0),
(206938,	2502,	'Mark Gomez',	'10155960928003433@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/10155960928003433',	'',	'2018-01-11 17:15:30',	'2018-01-11 23:15:30',	'Bob Ackerman sold me my first Selmer Mark VI Alto in 1985!  Also sold me my first Selmer Mark VI Soprano soon after!  Great memories visiting his large home/office with such a large selection!  Like a kid in a candy store!  :)',	0,	'1',	'SNAP',	'',	0,	0),
(206939,	1953,	'Raslan Fayad Abul Ardat',	'10155804656031675@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/10155804656031675',	'',	'2018-01-12 03:17:11',	'2018-01-12 09:17:11',	'I loved the idea and tried it my self',	0,	'1',	'SNAP',	'',	0,	0),
(206941,	6305,	'Scott Baiar',	'scott_f_baiar@yahoo.com',	'',	'24.22.52.98',	'2018-01-21 12:35:44',	'2018-01-21 18:35:44',	'Really great article about Michael. I\'m a semi-pro/pro tenor player here in Vancouver, Wa / Portland, Or. I met Michael in college (WSU) around \'87, when he was playing a gig in Spokane, Wa. We hung out and talked at a party he attended after the gig. There was hardly anybody talking to him at the party. It was so stupid. I think people were too afraid or didn\'t know what to say. So, the second I got the opportunity I was right there with him, just shootin\' the musical breeze. Truly the nicest guy ever. Was, and probably still is, the greatest inspiration to my playing. The world continues to be undoubtedly robbed by his passing. I miss his continuing contribution deeply. Your article touched my heart.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/58.0.3029.110 Safari/537.36 Edge/16.16299',	'',	0,	0),
(206942,	6305,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-01-21 20:20:50',	'2018-01-22 02:20:50',	'Scott:\r\n\r\nI\'m glad you liked my article.Michael was truly a Great guy not just a Great player. He was easy to talk to and very humble. I am so glad that you got a chance to hang w/him and talk to him. I hope you got a photo of you two together. Take care.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(206943,	6305,	'Scott Baiar',	'scott_f_baiar@yahoo.com',	'',	'24.22.52.98',	'2018-01-21 21:58:17',	'2018-01-22 03:58:17',	'Unfortunately I didn\'t get any shots. Didn\'t have cell phones with cameras back then. For that matter, they didn\'t have cell phones. lol But, I\'ll never forget it! Got to hang with Victor Lewis (Stan Getz drummer) for 3 weeks at the Sandpoint Jazz Festival, that was awesome... and got to play on stage in a combo format (2 horns w/full rhythm section) with Wynton Marsalis during that same festival. He was the one who asked if I wanted to play with him... how cool is that?! Those are my memories of fame. lol Still I\'ll never forget them and I learned a ton.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/58.0.3029.110 Safari/537.36 Edge/16.16299',	'',	206942,	0),
(206944,	6305,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-01-22 12:10:24',	'2018-01-22 18:10:24',	'Scott: It sounds like you\'ve had some good experiences.\r\n\r\nTake care,\r\n\r\nLarry W',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(206945,	6305,	'Joe Sullivan',	'sullivan_1@comcast.net',	'',	'73.191.100.92',	'2018-01-22 20:27:19',	'2018-01-23 02:27:19',	'Late reply.... but yes, I am the same guy but mostly play tenor. I am still teaching school and playing.\r\nThanks again for your article.\r\nBest,\r\nJoe',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_10_5) AppleWebKit/603.3.8 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/10.1.2 Safari/603.3.8',	'',	206896,	0),
(206946,	6350,	'Tony Lopez',	'Lopez4081@sbcglobal.net',	'',	'172.56.40.114',	'2018-01-23 10:00:56',	'2018-01-23 16:00:56',	'Thanks so much for the insight. I hear melodies in my head but just cannot seem to transfer that to my axe.As an exercise I sing a short two measure riff and try to play it back using my axe.Seems to be working. I also try that same riff in different keys.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 5.1.1; LG-K330 Build/LMY47V) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/61.0.3163.98 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(206947,	6305,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-01-23 11:20:47',	'2018-01-23 17:20:47',	'Joe: Where are you teaching, what school? What kind of gigs are you playing? I will be relocating to the Baltimore area in the next few years and I am already networking. Are you in the Union, do you play shows, have your own band? I played NYE w/a Wash DC based big band at the Bethesda Blues and Jazz Club. Take care.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	206945,	0),
(206948,	6350,	'Jon Gordon',	'JONGORDONMUSIC1@GMAIL.COM',	'https://jongordonmusic.com',	'24.78.93.190',	'2018-01-24 17:49:12',	'2018-01-24 23:49:12',	'Hey Tony, that sounds like a good way to do it. Get small pieces of things you\'re hearing on the horn. Take them through the keys. See how and where you can apply them in the context of basic harmony, ii, v, I\'s and tunes. But also try to get them at the piano and see if they can be the start of a composition. Sometimes for me it will just be an interval, like a 2nd, that will inspire a piece or an idea to develop when improvising. A lot of great pieces and improvisations start with a small idea, and the artistry in it isn\'t the idea itself so much as what you do with it...',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.10; rv:57.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/57.0',	'',	0,	0),
(206952,	3863,	'Woody Haiken',	'whaiken@gmail.com',	'',	'24.206.184.14',	'2018-02-01 00:03:30',	'2018-02-01 06:03:30',	'Thank you for this article. I tried using the EZO pads and it\'s worked like a charm. I made a template out of a credit card after measuring my teeth, and am able to get 5 from one pad. I wet a new one and then press it over my teeth. As I play, it molds itself neatly to my lower teeth. When I\'m done playing I rinse it, and then soak it in Listerine. One of these home-made protectors lasts for a couple of weeks. No more sore lower lip. The nice thing about the EZO pads is that they\'re thin, so that I didn\'t have to change my embrouchure. Mouthguards would be too thick, and much more expensive. Thanks for the suggestion!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_13_3) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/63.0.3239.132 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(206984,	1596,	'Jeff Wilson',	'wilsojeffrey@gmail.com',	'',	'172.194.187.84',	'2018-02-05 04:27:33',	'2018-02-05 10:27:33',	'Thanks',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPad; CPU OS 9_3_5 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/601.1.46 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/9.0 Mobile/13G36 Safari/601.1',	'',	347,	0),
(206994,	6365,	'Ernest',	'ernestazad@gmail.com',	'',	'14.192.208.229',	'2018-02-06 11:30:02',	'2018-02-06 17:30:02',	'Thanks',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPad; CPU OS 9_3_5 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/601.1.46 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/9.0 Mobile/13G36 Safari/601.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207002,	4854,	'David Suleiman',	'dsuleiman@berklee.edu',	'',	'76.24.8.252',	'2018-02-07 02:07:50',	'2018-02-07 08:07:50',	'Francois Louis is a very underrated mouthpiece Craftsman. I always get the same \"I didn\'t know he also made mpcs\" referring to his ligature work',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 8.1.0; Pixel 2 XL Build/OPM1.171019.013) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.137 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207007,	3863,	'Sarah Garrard',	'info@themusicroomsselby.co.uk',	'',	'81.140.208.243',	'2018-02-07 12:08:09',	'2018-02-07 18:08:09',	'Unfortunately I think it is a hazard of the instrument - however, what you do between playing songs (if playing for extended times) can alleviate some of the discomfort in the short term - It\'s not attractive, and hard to describe, but the \'horse impersonation\' (do you get what I mean?! blowing air through lips to relax them!) can be a life saver if you are on your last mouth hold! It has saved the end of many a set over time!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/58.0.3029.110 Safari/537.36 Edge/16.16299',	'',	0,	0),
(207058,	6305,	'Mike Durig',	'michaeldurig@verizon.net',	'',	'204.126.10.231',	'2018-02-12 10:39:30',	'2018-02-12 16:39:30',	'Nicely written Larry, Good job.  All my best.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; WOW64; Trident/7.0; Touch; rv:11.0) like Gecko',	'',	0,	0),
(207059,	6305,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-02-12 10:57:34',	'2018-02-12 16:57:34',	'Mike: Thank you. To bad you were out by then, you would have enjoyed thus day Mike. Hope your well amigo!!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	207058,	0),
(207082,	624,	'Harry',	'h_saich@hotmail.com',	'',	'60.229.84.172',	'2018-02-16 05:35:07',	'2018-02-16 11:35:07',	'+ Jerry Coker\'s \'Jazz Patterns\'',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.11; rv:58.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/58.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207125,	6412,	'John Plichta',	'jfp502@gmail.com',	'',	'24.127.83.214',	'2018-02-19 19:12:46',	'2018-02-20 01:12:46',	'I\'m a newbe, all those sharps and flats drive me up the wall.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.0) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/49.0.2623.112 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207126,	6305,	'Jeremy',	'jbrown@ucalgary.ca',	'',	'70.77.208.85',	'2018-02-19 20:50:15',	'2018-02-20 02:50:15',	'Thank you Larry, I enjoyed your interview with M. Brecker a lot.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.13; rv:58.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/58.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207128,	6412,	'Adrian Hopper',	'hoppers@freeuk.com',	'',	'143.159.71.118',	'2018-02-20 04:18:00',	'2018-02-20 10:18:00',	'Think in \'steps\' like the old session guys do - then a sharp or flat is just one step away from it\'s nearest whole note,don\'t forget that they\'re named differently whether ascending or descending.\r\nRegards, Another Newbie(ish)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64; rv:58.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/58.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207135,	6305,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-02-20 17:54:13',	'2018-02-20 23:54:13',	'Jeremy: Your welcome, I\'m glad you enjoyed it. Take care.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207136,	6412,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-02-20 18:10:00',	'2018-02-21 00:10:00',	'Bob: I am a tenor sax player too but I play some piano. I had to get past the piano test in college and just expanded it from there. \r\n\r\nI like to have musical conversations w/my drummer, it\'s a lot of fun. A few things I actually dislike. A) a bass player who does not know his role in the ensemble, playing lots of altered notes that are not called for in the tune, you know a guy who is playing # 11ths but never plays the root or 5th while the head and solos are happening. On his solo go ahead, knock yourself out. Also a bass player who plays to busy during the head of the tune. Here we are playing a simple blues and the bass player is running all over the place. B) a piano player that doesn\'t voice chords well and just plays to vanilla. C) a drummer who just plays time and doesn\'t setup the various sections of a tune. D) anyone who is not listening and just going on auto-pilot.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207140,	3866,	'Duncan Grant',	'duncan7868@talktalk.net',	'',	'89.241.131.112',	'2018-02-21 11:49:43',	'2018-02-21 17:49:43',	'Hi \r\nWhere did you get the photo of the lady singer and sax player from?\r\nRegards\r\nD',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 10_3_3 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/603.3.8 (KHTML, like Gecko) Mobile/14G60 [FBAN/FBIOS;FBAV/159.0.0.48.97;FBBV/91994325;FBDV/iPhone7,1;FBMD/iPhone;FBSN/iOS;FBSV/10.3.3;FBSS/3;FBCR/O2;FBID/phone;FBLC/en_GB;FBOP/5;FBRV/0]',	'',	0,	0),
(207141,	3866,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'70.113.101.140',	'2018-02-21 12:20:21',	'2018-02-21 18:20:21',	'Hello Duncan,\r\n\r\nI believe I got the photo from Flickr\'s Creative Commons selection. Is it your photo? \r\n\r\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_13_3) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.167 Safari/537.36',	'',	207140,	1),
(207184,	1852,	'Abe',	'avih777@gmail.com',	'',	'72.229.173.0',	'2018-02-26 21:49:56',	'2018-02-27 03:49:56',	'What mouthpiece and reeds?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 7.1.1; SM-N950U1 Build/NMF26X) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.137 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207208,	2576,	'Ron',	'Saxmantrevor64@aol.com',	'',	'97.81.83.121',	'2018-02-28 20:24:07',	'2018-03-01 02:24:07',	'Not Matt but can put in my 2 Cents as I just Purchased a Cannonball Raven Big Bell Stone series . I’ve played professionally 35 years taught even done some stretches in the repair business . I’ve played and collected every well respected brand of sax . I’ve played my main Ax a Selmer Mark VI Tenor I hand picked from 6 already hand selected tenors. I never thought I top my beloved Selmer tenors sound as well as feel . Last month I was killing time at a music store in Atlanta . I tried a Cannonball Tenor out just to kill time. I was literally blown away in Every way !  Went back  ( Long drive) the next week just to be sure my mind wasn’t playing tricks on me and I took my Selmer . Side by side there was no contest the Cannonball “ Kilked It “ all around . I purchased it on the spot ! The most amazing horn I have ever played or heard hands down .',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 11_1_1 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.3.5 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/11.0 Mobile/15B150 Safari/604.1',	'',	184595,	0),
(207211,	337,	'Trevor',	'Midiguana@aol.com',	'',	'97.81.83.121',	'2018-03-01 11:45:26',	'2018-03-01 17:45:26',	'Just a friendly sugestin to  Doron ,\r\n   I promise I’m just trying to be helpful , why don’t  you do a quick google search for some of these questions you are receiving. Can’t help but notice most of your answers are “ I don’t know “, “ sorry I can’t help  “ The internet has such a vast pile of saxophone knowledge just waiting on you to find it.  It would give you info for next time as well . Just when you put yourself out as a person to go to for advice and people are excited to take the time to write you and wait on an answer. There should be more info available from you . \r\nHonestly I’m not trying to be critical I always appreciate other sax guys sharing info with the new comers. \r\nI have played and collected saxophone for 45 years and I did it long before there was an internet . After I started looking up things online my knowledge base grew 1000%. Now I can identify almost any vintage sax by serial # and tell you the history behind it as well as the general consensus on its tone, best mouthpieces to go with it for different sounds etc .... all because I  googled every question I had. For so long . Now I write the articles others use as a reference . So why not try doing a quick search when you get in a questions so you can give more answers I think as a saxophone enthusiast you will also enjoy the knowledge you gain \r\n????????????\r\nTrevor',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 11_1_1 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.3.5 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/11.0 Mobile/15B150 Safari/604.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207240,	337,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//',	'70.113.101.140',	'2018-03-04 09:39:34',	'2018-03-04 15:39:34',	'Trevor, I actually agree with you 200%. This was one of the first articles I put up on the site as I thought it was something that folks would be really interested in. But the truth is, my passion lies in the music itself, and I\'ve really never been super-focused on gear, as I find it really uninspiring to spend time worrying about whether or not I am playing the best possible horn our mouthpiece I can play. Because of this, a few years ago I brought onboard a sax gear specialist, Zach Sollitto to handle all of the product reviews and gear-related articles. Zach is a walking encyclopedia of the latest brands and knows a ton about the mechanics of the instrument itself. \r\n\r\nSo please don\'t think that I am presenting myself as an expert in the area of sax gear, because I definitely don\'t consider myself that at all. \r\n\r\nLooking back at all of those \"Sorry, I don\'t know\" comments I left in reply to folks with questions on this article, I do see that my responses are probably really annoying for readers to read, so I deleted some of the ones that had no useful info. \r\n\r\nIf you ever have time or interest, it would be awesome if you replied to the comments, as my real goal is to foster a community where folks like you could fill in the blanks left by someone like me, whose expertise lies elsewhere. Regardless, thanks for the honest and super-constructive feedback!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_13_3) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.186 Safari/537.36',	'',	207211,	1),
(207242,	3863,	'Jonathan',	'jonathan@jc3dinc.com',	'',	'66.169.64.231',	'2018-03-04 10:21:30',	'2018-03-04 16:21:30',	'I never understood this. I have what many people tell me is really beautiful tone, and I do overtones every single day as my first warmup. That said without significant embouchure pressure I can\'t get the super high notes to sound. Anything above an alt A requires a lot of pressure. I play all my scales and exercises all the way up to alt F.  Playing that high requires A LOT of pressure.  A big part of that pressure comes from my lower jaw. I\'ve been trying for over 35 years to play with a \"relaxed\" embouchure up top, and it has never worked. I use a dentist made plastic tooth bleaching tray to protect my lower lip due to my aggressive altissimo playing. Would be lost in pain without it. So the relaxed embouchure is a big mystery to me. After 35 years of playing sax, I\'ve got some pretty intensely developed and strong musculature in my embouchure, how can that be developed from relaxing! The stronger these muscles get the more tonal control and beauty I have in my sound. I just don\'t get it. How can one devolop embouchure muscles by relaxing!!?? Please explain!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 8.1.0; Pixel 2 XL Build/OPM1.171019.018) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.137 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	3205,	0),
(207256,	6389,	'Heath Watts',	'heathdwatts@gmail.com',	'https://heathwatts.com',	'73.165.58.64',	'2018-03-05 21:17:20',	'2018-03-06 03:17:20',	'Thanks for these ideas, Glen. You might like Tunable too, if you haven’t found it already. It will play a drone and allow you to use the tuner simultaneously. The tuner lets you set the level of error that you prefer, for example, you can set the tuner so that if you play +/- 5 cents, you’re in the acceptable zone.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPad; CPU OS 11_2_5 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.5.6 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/11.0 Mobile/15D60 Safari/604.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207257,	6389,	'Mike',	'mrkin@btinternet.com',	'',	'86.138.162.140',	'2018-03-06 02:45:38',	'2018-03-06 08:45:38',	'I would definitely recommend forScore. I use it regularly- particularly when playing in my band.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 11_2_6 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.5.6 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/11.0 Mobile/15D100 Safari/604.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207261,	6389,	'Bill Siggelkow',	'bill.siggelkow@gmail.com',	'',	'107.77.68.16',	'2018-03-06 17:38:07',	'2018-03-06 23:38:07',	'2 tHat I like are iRealPro (real book chord changes and playalong) and Play By Ear for ear training.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 10_3_3 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/603.3.8 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/10.0 Mobile/14G60 Safari/602.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207278,	624,	'Jerry So',	'soyauchung@yahoo.com.hk',	'',	'202.155.232.194',	'2018-03-07 22:12:44',	'2018-03-08 04:12:44',	'Hello,\r\n\r\nThanks you so much for your list, I have studied Alto saxophone (play Classical mostly) around 2 yrs, however I would like start to learn and play about Jazz music and improvisation. \r\nWould you give me advise which suitable reference book?\r\n\r\nThanks again!\r\nJerry',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.186 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207292,	2531,	'sam moran',	'littlesaltysammie@icloud.com',	'',	'204.100.235.121',	'2018-03-09 14:48:05',	'2018-03-09 20:48:05',	'i wanted to know a bit more about your site but the information that was provided was good but i would like to know who the trumpet influenced the world but other then that i really enjoyed the site',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (X11; CrOS x86_64 10176.72.0) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.167 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207300,	1831,	'John Dalto',	'noncreativejohn@gmail.com',	'',	'84.53.96.28',	'2018-03-10 04:24:26',	'2018-03-10 10:24:26',	'Just thought I\'d leave my 2 cents...\r\nYes the breath is critical, but your first step is to actually pick the horn up in such a way that you don\'t contort yourself. Nearly every player I see suffers some kind of \'saxophonitis\' (over 50% weight on the right leg, left shoulder raised, right shoulder compressed, neck tense). Preventing that pattern will give you a chance to breathe. (Disclaimer: I study a lot of Alexander technique)',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 11_2_2 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.1.34 (KHTML, like Gecko) CriOS/63.0.3239.73 Mobile/15C202 Safari/604.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207325,	6448,	'Jesus Barron',	'barronwinds@hotmail.com',	'',	'189.177.83.207',	'2018-03-12 21:25:32',	'2018-03-13 03:25:32',	'Zach. Thank’s for your research.and thank,S again for sharing',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 11_2_6 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.5.6 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/11.0 Mobile/15D100 Safari/604.1',	'',	0,	0),
(207332,	6389,	'Matthew',	'matt@howtoplaythesax.com',	'https://HowToPlayTheSax.com',	'144.132.40.20',	'2018-03-14 06:44:59',	'2018-03-14 12:44:59',	'I will need to check out Reframe / Anytune Pro.... it looks like it could be handy for my students.  Thanks for the tip !\r\nOn a side note, I was disappointed a few years ago when my young daughters cello teacher recommended a string tuning app called \"G String\".  It\'s actually a really good tuning app, but the inuendo has rather obvious images.  There are things that a young person cannot unsee when trying to find that app ...!\r\nThanks,\r\nMatthew',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.186 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207394,	5793,	'Smitty',	'Fixradioshow@gmail.com',	'',	'73.107.223.202',	'2018-03-19 19:45:23',	'2018-03-20 01:45:23',	'I love the info and all the updating news that you guys offer. I know I will never get to  NAMM but I still enjoy playing my Sax. Even a old guy like me still has fun playing around here even if I am not ready for a solo show! I wish there was more info and pictures when they talk about mouth pieces and reeds. I (like all of us) are searching for that special mouth piece and that 1 reed that matches up and makes it easy to blow and get that great tone. I do know that there are some great pro\'s and I would love to see what they use. Both the mouth piece and matching reed, Keep up the good work guys!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/64.0.3282.186 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207395,	1831,	'Lorenzo',	'lorenzopace@yahoo.com',	'',	'97.90.54.89',	'2018-03-19 19:51:18',	'2018-03-20 01:51:18',	'Maddy,  You need not bite so hard. Perhaps your mouthpiece is too big for you.Check out the newest posts on breathing and breath support. Investigate long tone exersizes.  I personally have my lower lip curled outward and thus cannot be bitten. One needs to develop the embouchure muscles to do this. Above all get a mouthpiece that is easier to blow. Picking the right mouthpiece is one of the most difficult things about the saxophone thing. Keep blowin!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Trident/7.0; rv:11.0) like Gecko',	'',	205334,	0),
(207422,	3708,	'Hunter',	'Moehringhunter@gmail.com',	'',	'73.181.202.126',	'2018-03-21 23:11:32',	'2018-03-22 05:11:32',	'My new meyer 5m hard rubber alto mouthpiece is cut slightly off center. The right side of the tip is a bit higher than the left and it plays seemingly fine. I was wondering if you have had  any expierience with this problem?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 7.0; LG-M322 Build/NRD90U) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.109 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207472,	5675,	'Luke',	'lukepeterhawkins@hotmail.com',	'',	'5.65.122.38',	'2018-03-26 08:18:35',	'2018-03-26 14:18:35',	'Thank you so much for putting all this information out there!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.181 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207492,	6521,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-03-27 10:52:18',	'2018-03-27 16:52:18',	'The photos: # 1 I am playing the bari solo on the Buddy Rich version of \"Mercy, Mercy, Mercy\" at some high school in Minnesota. \r\n\r\n# 2 the infamous TGIF fast attack Variety/Dixie Band. I am the tenor sax/clarinet player. This band was one of my favorites. We played at retirement homes, schools, for handicapped kids etc. This is a band that did a lot of Good and helped to repair the world. These guys were a bunch of characters. We also functioned independently from the rest of the band on road trips. We even had our own van and would go out together after gigs.\r\n\r\n# 3 Myself posing at Mt. Rushmore near Rapid City, South Dakota. Then in front of our Band Bus. We had the same driver for all of our trips a guy named Bill. He took a lot of my Army Band  photos. The uniform I am wearing is the old Army Band Blues. Because of the shoulder boards it confused a lot of people. People thought we were officers and would salute us. Of course we would return the salute to further confuse them. The Army changed this uniform so now the band members have their rank on their sleeves. \r\n\r\n# 4 Part of the Fleet Jazz Ensemble either before or after a concert in Norfolk, VA. Reading from left to right. Michael Wells; keyboard, Jason Robertson; drums, Clint McClanahan; electric bass, myself, Larry Weintraub on bari sax.\r\n\r\n# 5 The Ft.Carson Big Band playing at the Glenn Miller Festival in Clarinda, Iowa. I am the tenor sax player to the far left as you look at the picture. I am just to the right on the trpts. This was a Great Trip. Clarinda, Iowa is Glenn\'s home town. On this trip we went from 1981 to 1940. We drove up to the old Denver Airport in Aurora, CO. Took a plane to Omaha, NE. Then we got on a bus bouncing along the Iowa countryside until we got to Clarinda after several hours. Down the street from our motel was an A &amp; W Drive - In complete w/car hops although not on roller skates. \r\n\r\nTo me the best part of being in a military band was the travel, the people you meet, the groups I played in and people I played with (for the most part).\r\n\r\nThanks for reading, stayed tuned for Part 2. Remember if you have questions e-mail me at LWeintraub1@cox.net',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207499,	6521,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-03-27 19:24:59',	'2018-03-28 01:24:59',	'One amendment to the Navy audition procedure that is not on their website. However in an e-mail to me the Audition Supervisor said that \"after they do open call auditions in Millington, TN and they still cannot find a qualified person, then they will do local auditions by appointment.\" The question is, do you really want to take the chance of them auditioning say 25 sax players for say maybe 4 spots and then making an appointment at your local Navy Band or would you rather be at the audition in Millington, TN just knowing that you are going to Wow them?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207506,	4406,	'Devin Garramone',	'Dbgarramone@gmail.com',	'',	'174.220.20.224',	'2018-03-28 12:43:50',	'2018-03-28 18:43:50',	'JR Monterose, \"the greatest tenor you never heard of\" from Utica, NY.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 7.0; SM-G950U Build/NRD90M) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/60.0.3112.116 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207510,	6521,	'Paul Colombo',	'Paulguitarcolombo@gmail.com',	'https://Paulcolombomusic.com',	'24.246.230.34',	'2018-03-28 21:23:29',	'2018-03-29 03:23:29',	'Great srticle Larry!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 11_2_6 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/604.5.6 (KHTML, like Gecko) Mobile/15D100 [FBAN/FBIOS;FBAV/164.0.0.56.96;FBBV/98434650;FBDV/iPhone9,2;FBMD/iPhone;FBSN/iOS;FBSV/11.2.6;FBSS/3;FBCR/Sprint;FBID/phone;FBLC/en_US;FBOP/5;FB',	'',	0,	0),
(207521,	6521,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-03-29 11:16:31',	'2018-03-29 17:16:31',	'Thanks Paul. Hope your well!!',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207539,	1932,	'Frank Piscioneri',	'jasminefranky@hotmail.com',	'',	'168.167.79.154',	'2018-03-31 11:51:33',	'2018-03-31 17:51:33',	'Wow that\'s great Sam ...I just see the amount of teaching hours you put in  ...one has to pay the bills but I\'m guessing students are fairly advanced which can be enjoyable !',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/58.0.3029.110 Safari/537.36 Edge/16.16299',	'',	0,	0),
(207540,	6525,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-03-31 12:16:40',	'2018-03-31 18:16:40',	'Some quick remarks. If you are a college student who plays sax and doubles flute or clarinet and you want to make some money this summer here is what I suggest. COntact Pro Ship at www.Proship.com NOW!! This is a good time to audition for a Summer gig from May -August.\r\n\r\nIf you happen to be traveling to Europe this summer whilst there pop in at some of the Public Radio Stations and talk to them about their audition requirements, scheduled openings etc, etc. Most Western and some Eastern European countries have some form of Socialized Music. These are bands run by and supported by the State. Check it out, their are Americans playing in these bands and living overseas as Ex-Pats. \r\n\r\nActually I have a friend from Va Bch who is a retired Army piano player. He married a German girl and she refused to live in the USA. So he went to live in Germany w/her. He receives a stipend every month from the German govt as piano player. So you see it can be done. If either of these options appeal to you then get moving on it ASAP. Just remember, while you are thinking about it, there is some other sax player who is acting on it now.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207556,	6525,	'Larry Weintraub',	'lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.174.58',	'2018-04-01 12:39:15',	'2018-04-01 18:39:15',	'Remember it takes some Moxie to leave your familiar surroundings. If you cannot support yourself playing in your town then get out there and make a move now!! There are options but it\'s all up to you.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.6; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0',	'',	0,	0),
(207561,	3827,	'David Mitera',	'Dmfunkyhorn@sbcglobal.net',	'',	'73.50.158.126',	'2018-04-01 19:09:17',	'2018-04-02 01:09:17',	'I know this is an old thread but as a few had already mentioned Paul Maslin and his entire staff at P.M. Woodwind in Evanston IL is the only place I frequent. Paul is the \"Guru\" of Saxophone repair and in terms of horns, if you can\'t find it there, you won\'t find it anyplace.\r\n\r\nHis expertise and customer service is second to none. He also has great workshops that take place with many well known Saxophone players from around the globe that do clinics there as well. I\'ve known Paul for 30 years and I\'m very fortunate that he is in my area. Even if he\'s not in your area it\'s most definitely worth the trip to come up or down to see him !',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 8.1.0; Pixel Build/OPM1.171019.021) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.109 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207562,	3708,	'mike',	'tfsushi@gmail.com',	'',	'47.33.120.99',	'2018-04-01 20:26:02',	'2018-04-02 02:26:02',	'Hello Dennis, thank you for this information.  I am reading this now in 2018, but see it was written in 2012.  I hope all is well and you are playing the same, smooth tenor as always.  If you still do tenor repair work, what city are you in?  As another reader has stated, taking your horn to different places, to find that one that was just adjusted has leaks or needs repair can be disappointing.  About finding issues on brand-new, good horns, like a Selmer, if you are not a top player, how can you be certain the horn you buy is the best of the ones there?  Do the shops not check each one?\r\nThank you again. mike',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.181 Safari/537.36',	'',	2619,	0),
(207566,	3708,	'Odd Evertsson',	'odd.evertsson@telia.com',	'',	'195.67.189.66',	'2018-04-02 04:10:01',	'2018-04-02 10:10:01',	'Hi!\r\nCalling from Sweden.\r\nThe sticky G# is a usual problem. Over here i´ve red on the internet that it depends on the holes chimney - not the pad.\r\nSince then I Always after playing put any paper between, move it. If it looks wet I put in a dry one.\r\nMore over - the holes´metal korrossion can be the problem.\r\nI use a 2000 wet grinding paper in that case.\r\nThe Eb hole  can be stucked if You don´t place a paper there after playing and put the sax in the case. \r\nEverything must be as dry as possible after playing.\r\nI also care for the octave keys and the Lowe C# key.\r\nThe Jupiter soprano is the worst one among my horns\r\nEven the clarinet gets this care. \r\nMy horns: 1953 Selmer alto, old Conn tenor, rather new soprano and an old very good Buffet clarinet.\r\nClose to me i visit often an hungarien educati\r\ned repairman.\r\nFor me isn´t pads the problem.\r\nI´ve played my horns since a I was 14 beeing 80 just now.\r\nMarching Bands,  jazz combos and Big Bands.\r\nPractise every day - rehearsing tomorrow with one of my three bands, New Orleans, jazz combo and the Big Band -\r\n10 gigs are waiting before midsummer.\r\n1',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1; WOW64; Trident/7.0; rv:11.0) like Gecko',	'',	2601,	0),
(207608,	4854,	'Grant King Koeller',	'gkoeller@woh.rr.com',	'',	'104.231.2.120',	'2018-04-03 19:13:36',	'2018-04-04 01:13:36',	'His name is Brian. not Brain .....',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (X11; CrOS x86_64 10323.62.0) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.184 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207609,	4854,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'107.201.109.91',	'2018-04-03 19:48:23',	'2018-04-04 01:48:23',	'Hello Grant,\r\n\r\nFixed. Thanks for catching that!\r\n\r\nBest,\r\n\r\nZach',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.181 Safari/537.36',	'',	207608,	15),
(207625,	3630,	'griffin elequin',	'griffinelequin@gmail.com',	'',	'97.84.244.125',	'2018-04-04 18:52:45',	'2018-04-05 00:52:45',	'so i have an issue! on my tenor, the secondary octave mechanism (the one that opens for d4-g4) is very close to the #f5 mechanism bar right above it and the pad\'s come unglued and sticks to the tone hole, making it impossible to play these notes without overtones. is there any way to fix this issue without taking off mechanisms and bars, or is this something i should just leave to a professional?',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 7.0; SM-G935P Build/NRD90M) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.109 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207653,	928,	'Phil Brenner',	'philbrenner3@gmail.com',	'',	'136.61.60.28',	'2018-04-06 07:05:42',	'2018-04-06 13:05:42',	'I would have to add Pete Christlieb Warne Marsh Apogee. Some great blowing on that one.',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android 4.4.2; SCH-I435 Build/KOT49H) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.109 Mobile Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
(207657,	4224,	'imagine',	'imaginerefrea@gmail.com',	'',	'76.235.21.80',	'2018-04-06 22:36:37',	'2018-04-07 04:36:37',	'Hi i am a one year in sax player and im wondering if anybody can have suggestions for me to improve?\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wD8itc7_wVY',	0,	'1',	'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/65.0.3325.181 Safari/537.36',	'',	0,	0),
