(229140,	7774,	'David Erato',	'david.erato@me.com',	'https://www.daviderato.com/blog/',	'68.23.158.114',	'2011-03-15 15:50:11',	'2011-03-15 22:50:11',	'Great stuff!  I guess the  Kenny G Audi commercial didn\'t quite make the cut.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229141,	7774,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'98.151.2.254',	'2011-03-15 21:45:31',	'2011-03-16 04:45:31',	'Hey David!\nThe Kenny G Audi commercial was cool, but somehow it didn\'t make me laugh like the ones I\'ve included here. But you\'re right to bring it up, as that video will definitely go down in viral saxophone video history.\nThanks for popping in,\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229140,	1),
(229142,	7774,	'A Dose of Friday Morning Saxo-Funny | Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever.',	'',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//a-dose-of-friday-morning-saxo-funny/',	'69.89.25.172',	'2011-06-03 03:01:46',	'2011-06-03 10:01:46',	'[...] few months ago I put out a post titled Viral Saxophone Videos You Can’t Afford to Miss. In a nutshell, the post was basically a roll-call of the funniest saxophone-related online videos [...]',	0,	'1',	'',	'pingback',	0,	0),
(229143,	7775,	'Rich Perry',	'richperry7@earthlink.net',	'',	'69.86.101.78',	'2015-12-29 09:57:20',	'2015-12-29 17:57:20',	'Thanks, I\'ve often wondered, \"what\'s his story?\"',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229144,	7777,	'Jeff Rzepiela',	'scooby@scooby-sax.com',	'https://www.scooby-sax.com',	'76.234.121.119',	'2011-07-14 06:29:00',	'2011-07-14 13:29:00',	'A great player and prolific transcriber!!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229145,	7777,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-14 07:52:53',	'2011-07-14 14:52:53',	'Thanks Jeff, I completely agree!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229144,	1),
(229146,	7777,	'Stephen',	'sjmsjmsjm@comcast.net',	'',	'68.36.62.233',	'2011-07-14 20:08:37',	'2011-07-15 03:08:37',	'Hey Doron,\nNice article on Charles. Although I am a trumpet player\nI love his transcriptions and exercises pdfs. By the way I live in New Jersey.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229147,	7777,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-14 21:34:31',	'2011-07-15 04:34:31',	'Hey Stephen,\nGreat to have a brass man visiting this slice of saxo-land! With solos, it really doesn\'t matter what instrument is being transcribed. In fact, sometimes I think it\'s better to play solos from other instruments as you end up with lines that don\'t naturally fall under the fingers for your instrument thus expanding your musical vocabulary.\nJersey, wow, I have a lot of memories of Greek diners out there, good times...\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229146,	1),
(229148,	7777,	'Bill Plake',	'bplake@earthlink.net',	'',	'99.109.197.253',	'2011-07-15 08:10:28',	'2011-07-15 14:10:28',	'Charles McNeal is a very generous person for sharing so readily his work. Though I look at the act of transcription as primarily (for me, not necessarily for others) an exercise in improving my ear (and therefore prefer to make my own transcriptions), I still find value in playing transcriptions made by others (sight reading, technical challenges, etc.) For that reason I visit his Site from time to time. Amazing his output! One of the things he said in the interview about singing each transcribed solo should be an essential practice for anyone studying and/or transcribing a solo. Lennie Tristano, Warne Marsh, Lee, Konitz, et. al., always adhered to this practice. It helped them to really internalize and hear very, very clearly what the soloist was doing. Anyhow, thanks for such an informative interview.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229149,	7777,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-16 10:57:17',	'2011-07-16 17:57:17',	'Hello Bill,\nYes, \"generous\" is most certainly the right word when it comes to describing Charles. I know that I\'ve gotten tremendous benefit out of transcribing solos myself, especially studying how with the great players, one idea develops out of the one before it. Really, there is so, so much to learn from this practice.\nIn any event, I\'m so glad that you enjoyed the interview, hope you continue to stop by the site.\nBest,\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229148,	1),
(229150,	7777,	'dylan',	'dylanheaney@gmail.com',	'',	'68.161.112.251',	'2011-07-20 22:23:52',	'2011-07-21 05:23:52',	'cool article, and charles\' website seems like a great resource. Anybody else have any trouble accessing the transcriptions though? I\'ve tried opening and downloading them in Chrome and Firefox with no success.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229151,	7777,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-07-21 08:17:22',	'2011-07-21 15:17:22',	'Hello Dylan,\nI just downloaded one of his transcriptions successfully, so I\'m not sure why you might be having problems. I would think that right-click on the link (making sure that your mouse is truly over the link so that the hand pointer appears) to the PDF and saving would do the trick.\nAlso, I\'m not sure if you already know this, but you need to have Adobe Acrobat installed in order to see these.\nI hope that helps,\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229150,	1),
(229152,	7778,	'TenorMoxie',	'geophone@hotmail.com',	'',	'75.110.194.6',	'2013-01-16 19:44:32',	'2013-01-17 03:44:32',	'Trane developed his unique embouchure to compensate for dental problems.\nThe single lip embouchure is the easiest way to get precise control, but it really takes away from the resonance of the reed.  Maybe it\'s good for certain ensembles and classical playing, but getting rid of it (after switching from clarinet) was necessary for me to get a good full sound.  I suspect that it is taught to a lot of beginning students because it is the quickest way to overcome certain beginner problems (particularly with really soft reeds), or because a lot of reed teachers (particuarly in school music programs) don\'t really appreciate the differences between clarinets and saxophones.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229153,	7779,	'Name',	'Example@gmail.com',	'https://www.example.com',	'199.235.194.99',	'2015-05-21 09:38:33',	'2015-05-21 16:38:33',	'Put Comment Here',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229154,	7779,	'sam moran',	'littlesaltysammie@icloud.com',	'',	'204.100.235.121',	'2018-03-09 14:48:05',	'2018-03-09 20:48:05',	'i wanted to know a bit more about your site but the information that was provided was good but i would like to know who the trumpet influenced the world but other then that i really enjoyed the site',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229155,	7780,	'Sam Sadigursky',	'sadigursky@gmail.com',	'http://www.SamSadigursky.com',	'207.38.131.12',	'2011-08-22 16:10:31',	'2011-08-22 23:10:31',	'Matt is hands down one of the finest repairman out there, and definitely somebody of rare integrity. Thanks for sharing these insights. There\'s definitely more than meets the eye to these horns, and sending a student out to buy a new horn is not so simple these days. Everybody should really do their research, and make sure somebody that plays gives anything a try before buying it!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229156,	7780,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-08-22 21:05:13',	'2011-08-23 04:05:13',	'Whoa, cool that you know Matt! Although I haven\'t had the pleasure of having him work on one of my horns, I can definitely agree about the integrity and in depth knowledge he has. Glad you dug the article. :-)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229155,	1),
