(229227,	7787,	'daniel',	'daniel@invincible.se',	'https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=jeopeo.musiciansmetronome',	'89.160.58.213',	'2013-07-05 02:14:07',	'2013-07-05 09:14:07',	'Another good alternative for android is Jeos Groove Metronome\nhttps://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=jeopeo.musiciansmetronome',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229223,	0),
(229228,	7787,	'jake',	'jeopeo@hotmail.com',	'',	'83.189.184.119',	'2013-07-12 13:09:23',	'2013-07-12 20:09:23',	'Thx daniel, I really like that one. You can even adjust \"swingrate\". Im a bassplayer and play a lot of jazz, and its really awesome to practise to the swingride in different tempos when u can adjust the swing.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229227,	0),
(229229,	7787,	'John Nastos',	'john@johnnastos.com',	'https://johnnastos.com',	'98.246.77.53',	'2013-07-12 13:58:56',	'2013-07-12 20:58:56',	'Metronomics for Android is now available, too.  It has all of the regular Metronomics features, including the adjustable swing rate one of the other commenters mentioned.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229223,	0),
(229230,	7787,	'jake',	'jeopeo@hotmail.com',	'',	'83.189.184.119',	'2013-07-12 17:46:38',	'2013-07-13 00:46:38',	'Cool app, random rythms can sound good.\nI like how easy it is in JEOS metronome to play uneven timesignatures and that u get allready preset grooves. Its easier.\nLike if u wanna practice samba there\'s a sambagroove ready for u and it plays it in 7/8 or whatever. Very neat.\nBut I like the random feature of your app, I bought both.\n:)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229229,	0),
(229231,	7788,	'Toni Linder',	'toni.linder@me.com',	'',	'178.194.113.28',	'2012-08-22 08:53:32',	'2012-08-22 15:53:32',	'I\'n not sure whether the difference in sound is worth $ 775 ...',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229232,	7788,	'Charles McNeal',	'charles@charlesmcneal.com',	'',	'68.224.135.51',	'2012-08-22 15:34:55',	'2012-08-22 22:34:55',	'To much distortion in the recording to get a clear idea of the difference in sound. But I\'ll agree with Toni, the difference that I hear is not worth $775...sorry.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229233,	7788,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-08-22 17:17:16',	'2012-08-23 00:17:16',	'Like all gear changes, the effect is usually much more perceived by the player than actually heard. *Feeling* like you sound better goes a long way, but it\'s a question of how much do you want to pay for something that makes you feel like you\'re sounding better when you can find something that might actually make you sound a bit better.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229232,	1),
(229234,	7788,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'http://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'144.160.130.16',	'2012-08-24 05:47:15',	'2012-08-24 11:47:15',	'Hey Toni and Charles, I apologize for the sound quality. I am going to re-record myself playing both mouthpieces sometime soon which I hope will help you as well as other players hear a difference between the pieces.I found both pieces to play great and share many of the same features which is why the difference might not seem so significant. The one thing about the theo wanne is you can purchase one today unlike the old otto link hard rubber mouthpieces which you have to go on ebay, go to a speciality shop, or know of someone who is selling one through word of mouth. I apologize again for the sound quality, but I hope the article kept you up to date on what theo wanne has to offer.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229235,	7788,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-08-28 12:52:50',	'2012-08-28 19:52:50',	'For whoever\'s following this thread, Zach was kind enough to record the mouthpieces using much better equipment, and I think that the difference between the 2 pieces is a lot more obvious now. Give \'em a listen!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	1),
(229236,	7788,	'David Lee',	'kangsterk@live.com',	'',	'69.127.168.9',	'2012-10-08 17:19:31',	'2012-10-09 00:19:31',	'Hey, any higher quality is worth the pay.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229237,	7788,	'zahiir',	'cruzonsax@yahoo.com',	'',	'65.34.175.238',	'2012-10-09 11:59:06',	'2012-10-09 18:59:06',	'Why is it that most artists that endorse these producuts don\'t necessarily use them?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229238,	7788,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'http://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'139.76.64.65',	'2012-10-09 12:20:52',	'2012-10-09 18:20:52',	'Hey zahiir,\nI know Tim Price and a couple artist\'s play theo\'s pieces all the time. Some artist are very picky with what mouthpiece they play and like to change there setup frequently. From my understanding, an artist might play one mouthpiece for a couple of months and than switch to another one quickly. Is there one artist in particular that you see frequently switching to different mouthpieces?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229239,	7788,	'zahiir',	'cruzonsax@yahoo.com',	'',	'65.34.175.238',	'2012-10-10 12:37:15',	'2012-10-10 19:37:15',	'Well I was wondering what the endorsement really means...ie. did they try it and say oh this is good or whatever..It just sounds like a selling gimmick, which if its the case is not very honest.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229238,	0),
(229240,	7788,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'http://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'139.76.64.66',	'2012-11-05 14:08:46',	'2012-11-05 20:08:46',	'Hey Zahiir,\nI can understand where you are coming from in regards to endorsements. As you probably know with many players, they like to change their setup every so often. You might see one player playing on a mouthpiece for a couple months and then the next time be playing on something totally different. I view these endorsements as many players actually play on theo wanne\'s mouthpieces as well as many pro\'s are stating that theo wanne makes a great mouthpiece which is true.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229239,	0),
(229241,	7788,	'richard ross',	'richardsaxross@yahoo.com',	'https://richardsaxross.com',	'108.74.175.49',	'2016-10-24 06:12:18',	'2016-10-24 13:12:18',	'great jazz sound on both!, But the Amma sounds more refined, fatter and darker, better in tune and as if it\'s easier to control and articulate',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229242,	7789,	'Jeff Rzepiela',	'scooby@scooby-sax.com',	'https://www.scooby-sax.com',	'76.232.11.109',	'2011-11-21 08:05:14',	'2011-11-21 16:05:14',	'Very nice! I think I\'ll print these out and tape them on the wall of my practice room.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229243,	7789,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-11-21 08:12:20',	'2011-11-21 16:12:20',	'That\'s awesome Jeff! There are definitely some nuggets here, so hopefully these help keep you even more inspired.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229242,	1),
(229244,	7789,	'Joe Wagner',	'joeytwagner@yahoo.com',	'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vhfTCKPjjMY',	'163.251.239.3',	'2011-11-25 05:43:23',	'2011-11-25 13:43:23',	'Thanks Doron,\nThese quotes are great. I really like the one by David Sanborn about playing the right notes and learning to leave well enough alone, it really is a lifelong quest. Great site!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229245,	7789,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-11-26 13:13:08',	'2011-11-26 21:13:08',	'Hey there Joe,\nIf anyone knows how to leave out extraneous stuff from their solos and melodic interpretation, it\'s Sanborn.\nAnd so glad that you like the site, it\'s always great to get positive feedback.\n:-)\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229244,	1),
(229246,	7789,	'Dan Meinhardt',	'danmeinhardt@gmail.com',	'https://www.danmeinhardt.com',	'75.34.16.108',	'2013-02-01 19:19:12',	'2013-02-02 03:19:12',	'You know Doron, these viewpoints are really narrow-minded (just jazz? Where\'s all the Norwegian death metal?) and I feel like my musical concept hasn\'t expanded one bit. You said to let you know.\nBut seriously, this was just another in a long line of fantastic posts and insights from BSWE. Definitely opened my mind and reminded me of plenty to keep in mind. Keep up the great work!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229247,	7789,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-02-02 23:12:53',	'2013-02-03 07:12:53',	'Oh man, are you another one of those Norwegian Death Metal nazi - like if\'s it\'s not Norwegian Death Metal it\'s not music? I wonder if you even know who the heavies in Norweigian Death Metal are. I mean, when\'s the last time you transcribed a Varg \'Count Grishnackh\' Vikernes of Burzum\'s tunes and memorized them in all twelve keys? In case you need a lesson: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Norwegian_black_metal_scene\nOK, I obviously have too much time on my hands for a Saturday night, but I do thank you, good sir, for the kind words, it\'s very inspiring to hear. :)\nNow back to sacrificing a baby squirrel to make Kjøttboller out of.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229246,	1),
(229248,	7789,	'Mike (UK)',	'mtkin@btinternet.com',	'',	'81.159.86.155',	'2013-02-06 09:18:36',	'2013-02-06 17:18:36',	'I love the comedy sketch where the composer approaches the pianist and says, \'You\'re playing all the wrong notes\'. To which the pianist replies, I\'m playing all the right notes but not in the correct order\'.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229249,	7789,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-02-06 12:48:07',	'2013-02-06 20:48:07',	'Sounds like the pianist had a good point!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229248,	1),
(229250,	7789,	'Jon Trantham',	'trantham@yahoo.com',	'',	'173.31.162.206',	'2013-04-07 20:33:52',	'2013-04-08 03:33:52',	'I think you missed a couple.  Here are quotes &amp; songs from my two favorite sax players:\nPaul Desmond: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5RpfoH0drZ8\n\"Writing is like jazz. It can be learned, but it can\'t be taught.\" - Paul Desmond\nJunior Walker:\n\"I was different, that\'s all. Instead of singing with my voice, I sang with my horn.\" - Jr. Walker\n(Skip to 2:38)\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZRf8VV_iIwo&amp;list=PLsVVMTg65X98uC-gxqlpFE0Pvml0qeXjr&amp;index=4\nor watch the first 20 seconds of shotgun.  Amazing how a 20 second riff can have such a musical influence:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YnhI_ECOAK4',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229251,	7789,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-04-07 20:40:40',	'2013-04-08 03:40:40',	'These are great, thanks for sharing, Jon!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229250,	1),
(229252,	7789,	'Douglass Jones',	'douglass.jones31@gmail.com',	'https://www.facebook.com',	'58.111.106.86',	'2018-01-04 20:25:18',	'2018-01-05 02:25:18',	'I leaned a great leason years ago. Diversity is the key ! Call it what you like but I call it improvisation !',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229253,	7789,	'Bianca Jaybian Daneel Cloete',	'1661548387222082@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/1661548387222082',	'',	'2018-01-04 12:02:55',	'2018-01-04 18:02:55',	'Christian Bird',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229254,	7789,	'Christian Bird',	'1767738989951832@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/1767738989951832',	'',	'2018-01-04 12:09:36',	'2018-01-04 18:09:36',	'Nice..????',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229253,	0),
(229255,	7789,	'Susan Holcomb Sermons',	'2039829432701547@facebook.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/2039829432701547',	'',	'2018-01-04 11:23:03',	'2018-01-04 17:23:03',	'Samantha Sermons',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229256,	7790,	'TJ',	'tijmen_janssen@hotmail.com',	'',	'77.248.2.33',	'2012-10-05 18:02:13',	'2012-10-06 01:02:13',	'When I look at the video, it seems as if he\'s trying to tell that he believes reeds (in this case from Vandoren) need to mature in moisture. So, according to the steps above, it\'s all about making them wet and keeping them wet enough (hence the small amount of water left inside the jar) but not soaking wet to give them time to mature (and the taking out part is to test them and rank them and clean them).\nAnd then leaving them tip down in water to be able to take them out and play without having to make them moist before use (in case of breaking the current reed on his mouthpiece).\nThat\'s what I made of it.\nAnother tip I can give you, is to check during this process wether the reed stays completely flat while very moist. Some reeds tend to curve a little, which makes it seal less on the mouthpiece. (not the tip, but the bottom half of the reed right up to below the heart of the reed at the back side) Try flattening that part of the reed, without sanding off the vibrating part of the reed, with something like the glass reed resurfacer from Vandoren (just an example).',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229257,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-10-07 14:19:26',	'2012-10-07 21:19:26',	'I agree TJ. I know that many people swear by keeping your reeds completely submerged in water or mouth wash, but this is a slightly different approach.\nWhat you\'re suggesting sounds intriguing as well. I guess it\'s up to us to be \"mad scientists\" and experiment with what works best for us.\nThanks for chiming in!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229256,	1),
(229258,	7790,	'Kenneth',	'kennethkid10@gmail.com',	'',	'67.188.213.17',	'2012-11-19 22:11:36',	'2012-11-20 04:11:36',	'Is this method of reed preperation for a more edgy jazzy tone? Or does this method work well for a round, rich, dark classical, plus, edgy bright jazzy tone?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229259,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-11-19 22:51:23',	'2012-11-20 06:51:23',	'I don\'t see this as having anything to do with tonal color. I think it\'s more about developing a responsive reed, and a responsive reed is going to be a great plus for anyone, regardless of the style of music they\'re playing. :)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229258,	1),
(229260,	7790,	'Jim Kroll',	'jmk55@comcast.net',	'',	'71.239.86.181',	'2013-03-12 10:06:50',	'2013-03-12 17:06:50',	'After having the reed on my mouthpiece for about two weeks(wich I really enjoyed having it set up all the time because it played the same al the time) I started getting a little blue streak mold I\'m sure on the reed. Whats the best way to deal with this? Jim',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229261,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-03-12 21:26:17',	'2013-03-13 04:26:17',	'Hmm, well, leaving a wet reed out in the relative open like that is pretty much guaranteed to result in a moldy reed. That\'s why Sanborn and a lot of other guys leave the reeds submerged in water, and in fact, some players keep the reeds submerged in mouthwash or liquor of some sort. Otherwise, I don\'t think that there\'s any way around the mold as long as you\'re leaving the reed on the mouthpiece.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229260,	1),
(229262,	7790,	'Jim',	'jmk55@comcast.net',	'',	'71.239.86.181',	'2013-03-13 10:38:22',	'2013-03-13 17:38:22',	'I thought Sanborn said once he put a reed on he didnt take it off until it quit playing and that he covered it and wraped it to keep it moist and playing every day helped to keed it moist',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229263,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-03-18 11:31:38',	'2013-03-18 18:31:38',	'Duh, of course, I got mixed up with another reed preparation/storage procedure! Anyhow, I\'m not quite sure how to avoid the mold, but if it is building up, you could always scratch it off every few days (?).',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229262,	1),
(229264,	7790,	'Matt Doran',	'matt.doran@att.net',	'',	'70.183.196.235',	'2013-04-05 08:38:59',	'2013-04-05 15:38:59',	'During the initial soaking try a sprinkle of baking soda.  It is a mold killer and may prevent mold from forming as quickly...',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229260,	0),
(229265,	7790,	'Javi',	'saxpack23@gmail.com',	'https://www.facebook.com/javiercavacini',	'187.233.79.245',	'2013-05-12 20:43:58',	'2013-05-13 03:43:58',	'That´s really good information, thanks a lot.. But I got a doubt.. If you leave the reed inside the mouthpiece, with water, it means that the metal/rubber mouthpiece is with water too ALL the time while you are not playing? I´m not sure if I understood it right :/  I saw Sanborn taking the neck (with MP and reed) out of his case with a bag.. I guess it has water in it?\n  Thanks a lot! (English is not my languaje, as you can see, ja)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229266,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'4.31.55.178',	'2013-05-13 14:48:31',	'2013-05-13 21:48:31',	'Hello Javi, I don\'t think he was saying that you leave the reed attached to the mouthpiece and then leave the mouthpiece and reed in the water. What I got is that once you have soaked your reeds and have picked one you want to play on, you simply leave that reed on your mouthpiece and pack it away in your case like you normally would. The only time you\'re leaving anything in water is when you\'re preparing the reeds, but once you\'re actually playing on a reed, it doesn\'t get put in water again. I hope that helps!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229265,	1),
(229267,	7790,	'Javi',	'saxpack23@gmail.com',	'https://javiercavacini.com',	'187.233.79.245',	'2013-05-27 10:49:18',	'2013-05-27 17:49:18',	'Thanks, bro! But the reed wouldn´t get like twisted in the top? I dont know if I´m explaining correctly. Like when you get a used reed you have to smash it..',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229266,	0),
(229268,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'4.31.55.178',	'2013-05-28 13:00:57',	'2013-05-28 20:00:57',	'I suppose you\'d need to find a pretty big mouthpiece cap with plenty of room in the front.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229267,	1),
(229269,	7790,	'javi',	'saxpack23@gmail.com',	'',	'187.233.79.245',	'2013-06-09 19:15:40',	'2013-06-10 02:15:40',	'Hi! I´ve doing what Sanborn says, but at the third day all my reeds start smelling weird, like if they are about to get fungus, and i found them a little bit dark :/ Now I put all of them to dry, but their tips are veery twisted.. What do you recommend? Help! I did it with 15 reeds :/\n Thanks a lot!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229268,	0),
(229270,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-06-11 15:20:59',	'2013-06-11 22:20:59',	'Javi, did you leave the reeds you weren\'t currently playing inside the damp jar (with just a little bit of water in it)? There should only be one reed that stays outside of that jar.\nAdmittedly, this is a pretty unusual method. I personally just keep my reeds in a Rico Reedgard. You might also want to experiment with keeping your reeds completely submerged in a water proof container filled with mouth wash - a lot of great players swear by this method.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	1),
(229271,	7790,	'Javi',	'saxpack23@gmail.com',	'',	'187.233.79.245',	'2013-06-11 16:08:03',	'2013-06-11 23:08:03',	'Yes, i put the reeds that I were not using in the jar, but submerged in water.. I red that mixing 50% wate 50% vodka is better, so I tried it and apparently is working :)\n Now I left the mouthpiece with the reed and the cap, in a ziploc bag, and it keeps the humidity, and the vodka taste, ja.\n I´ll tell you later what happened. Thanks a lot for helping us. Cheers!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229270,	0),
(229272,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-06-11 19:45:52',	'2013-06-12 02:45:52',	'So glad to hear that you\'re getting better results, happy if I helped in any way!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229271,	1),
(229273,	7790,	'Luke Graham',	'Lgraham311@gmail.com',	'',	'66.86.166.64',	'2015-09-20 10:22:49',	'2015-09-20 17:22:49',	'What on Earth is a Reed well, and where on Earth do I find one to buy?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229274,	7790,	'Ade',	'indicajak@gmail.com',	'',	'82.45.62.33',	'2016-09-07 00:13:41',	'2016-09-07 07:13:41',	'Luke; same place as the klangnbogen, bright and dark thumb hooks, Ishimori stones, altissimo shoelaces....forget it. All that hassle to sound like a turbocharged kazoo !!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229275,	7790,	'Bill Easley',	'WilliamEasley@aol.com',	'http://BillEasley.Net',	'98.26.53.150',	'2018-05-25 17:52:11',	'2018-05-25 23:52:11',	'I keep a few ready to play reads in the Ziploc bag with a damp sponge . Use a file to fine tune the rough spots on front and back.  This week I am trying something sugested by Mark Vinci.   He stores rieeds soaking in white vinegar in the refrigerator.  I am only trying it because I think he’s brilliant .   This Reed stuff is kind a new to me since I’ve only been playing for 60 some  Years.  Proud and happy to be a Vandoren endorcer.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229276,	7790,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'70.113.101.140',	'2018-05-26 16:15:52',	'2018-05-26 22:15:52',	'Hello Bill, an honor to have you commenting here! Interesting method - hopefully the reeds don\'t smell too bad after soaking in that stuff, but I\'d be curious to give it a try. Thanks for sharing that!\nPS. Although I have not been bestowed the title of \"endorser\", I\'ve used Vandoren\'s myself nearly my entire playing career, they\'re among the least-bad reeds I\'ve ever played. :)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229275,	1),
(229277,	7791,	'Ruston',	'rustonreynolds@gmail.com',	'http://Windsmithmusic.com',	'174.224.19.245',	'2019-07-31 12:28:41',	'2019-07-31 17:28:41',	'I’m getting about 3-4 hours out of their Black Bamboo and Hinoki reeds before they start curling towards the tip. Loved the tone, but can justify spending 5x’s the cost for a reed that lasts a fraction of the time a cane reed does. I’ve written the company about my experience and that of several other players I know. No response yet. Google tells me this experience is common. Too bad. Now if they were $2-3 each, I’d play on one a couple of times, toss it and feel good about it.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229278,	7793,	'Dave Erato',	'david.erato@me.com',	'https://www.daviderato.com',	'24.209.132.81',	'2013-02-28 11:23:55',	'2013-02-28 19:23:55',	'Keilwerth does have a website, however no mention of the new horn.  Their marketing people should be fired.  I did a blog post with pictures and the videos they\'ve posted on their facebook page.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229279,	7793,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-02-28 14:58:04',	'2013-02-28 22:58:04',	'Oh wow, OK. They definitely could use some help in the online marketing and SEO department. Can you please post the link here? Thanks Dave!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229278,	1),
(229280,	7793,	'Craig Edgar',	'Craigedgarsax@gmail.com',	'',	'70.138.225.47',	'2013-02-28 19:16:28',	'2013-03-01 03:16:28',	'Did you get a chance to play the Tenor Madness Custom horns? I stopped by their booth at TMEA a couple weeks ago and was completely blown away. My main horn is a \'56 Martin and it was the first time I\'d ever thought of moving to something else. I\'d be curious as to what your thoughts are on the TM custom if you got a chance to play it at all.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229281,	7793,	'Dave Erato',	'david.erato@me.com',	'https://www.daviderato.com',	'24.209.132.81',	'2013-03-01 06:00:22',	'2013-03-01 14:00:22',	'https://www.daviderato.com/blog/2013/1/28/new-keilwerth-mkx-horns.html',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229279,	0),
(229282,	7793,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'139.76.224.66',	'2013-03-01 09:36:14',	'2013-03-01 17:36:14',	'Hey Craig,\nI have spoken with Randy and at some point will meet with him to test play his TM custom tenors. I have heard great things about these tenors and I am interested in checking out the un-lacquered model as well as the gold plated model. I should have a review on Randy\'s line of horns in the upcoming months.\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229280,	0),
(229283,	7793,	'tom',	'tomjiujitsu@yahoo.com',	'',	'68.38.140.166',	'2013-03-25 20:40:55',	'2013-03-26 03:40:55',	'you forgot the new Yamaha\'s 62iii and 82zii for both alto and tenor.  I can\'t wait to try them out.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229284,	7793,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'139.76.64.67',	'2013-03-26 06:35:39',	'2013-03-26 13:35:39',	'Hey Tom,\nI wish I had a bit more time to check out Yamaha\'s new line up of horns at the NAMM show. Since their booth was located in the Marriot and was seperate from the main show, I was not able to get over there. I am planning on review the Yamaha custom 82Z some time later this year. I will keep you posted when receive the horn.\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229285,	7793,	'tom',	'tomjiujitsu@yahoo.com',	'',	'63.234.171.106',	'2013-03-26 07:28:13',	'2013-03-26 14:28:13',	'Thanks Zach,\nI just got a new silver yas-82z last month, but i\'m drooling over the new one. I can\'t wait to hear your review.  Thank you so much for an awsome site.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229286,	7793,	'John Bayne',	'`johnbayne@ihug.co.nz',	'',	'203.173.196.143',	'2013-04-16 00:13:28',	'2013-04-16 06:13:28',	'I went to Taiwan early this year to visit the person who made me my dream tenor saxophone back in 2010. It was the Selmer Paris Super 80 Series III A64 design, but in 0.8mm instead of 0.7mm solid sterling silver and I had my good friend and Technician Tim polish the inside bore and another good friend and electroplater extraordinaire, Dennis electroplate one micron of gold inside only, of the body, bow and bell, with the gold plate join to the solid sterling silver hidden where the bell bends over to meet the outside body. This saxophone beats everything which I have ever played because of the extra seventh thickness in solid sterling silver and the gold plating on the inside, but I am now convinced that my Kind Super 20 Silversonic, is a better design as regards the larger than life tone, especially the lower register, and so, using my dream sax as a launch pad for even better tenor saxophones, which I hope to sell, complete with, what is even more important, the best mouthpieces in the world, for the mouthpiece is like the engine of a sports car or motorbike.\nI am convinced that solid copper in 0.9mm will not weigh as much as solid sterling silver in 0.8mm for the reason that, as far as the neck, body, bow and bell is concerned, it is  only one eighth heavier, whereas the specific gravity of sterling silver, at 10.36gm/cc is slighty more than an eight heavier for copper is 9.1gm/cc. ie: 9x9.1=81.9, whereas 10.36x8=82.88. I do not care at all about increased weight but more players do than don\'t so I have to make it light as possible. If there is say 16oz of keys and octave mechanism and brass is 7.4 and I use titanium instead at 4.54 we have a saving of 16 x 4.54 divided by 7.4=7oz saving. So I am hoping by next year to have a King Super 20 Silversonic/Coppersonic in 0.9mm with 0.8mm of solid copper and 0.03mm of copper plating inside (polished bore of course) and out, and 0.02mm of silver plating inside and out (which gets rid of copper\'s only unacceptable problem (in the past) of absorbing too much sound like copper absorbs too much gold if plated directly. I am doing my best to have the vastly superior keys action of my 0.8mm solid sterling silver sax on my King without the twist to the left of my King as well. If this can be done, it really will be a huge step up yet again over my initial dream saxophone. Last comparison of my 0.8mm was when my friend Roger played firstly his Selmer Paris Super Balanced action which was certainly one of the most vibrant of saxophones, and then mine. When he played his it sounded like a good live sax like my King, but when he played my prototype it sounded so polished like he was playing an expensive microphone through a great PA with the best pre amp set up, as opposed to the horrible acoustics of a normal home lounge room.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229287,	7793,	'William Bua',	'bua6969@comcast.net',	'',	'76.114.42.142',	'2013-05-15 19:40:49',	'2013-05-16 02:40:49',	'Hey Zack\nI am the proud owner of a TM custom and until you try one, you haven\'t played the best modern tenor. I am lucky enough to have tried over 20 necks from  3 different sound concepts Randy had to offer.  Of course the TM\'s are set up perfectly (that goes without saying) but the sound!  I am a horn flipper and I play everything other than the Italian horns and the new Mantra and this TM beats them all. I have only played 3 horns that play better and they were pre mark VI Selmers.  I had a ref 36 not long ago.  I bought it cheap and then checked everything with a light and put it up against the TM and it wasn\'t even close.  Since you play a lot of horns and review them, I would love to hear your take on these horns.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229282,	0),
(229288,	7793,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'139.76.224.66',	'2013-05-16 07:28:07',	'2013-05-16 14:28:07',	'Hey William,\nI have talked to Randy and am hoping to demo these horns some time soon. If I visit New York soon, I will stop by KB saxophone services to demo these saxophones. I have heard great things about these tenors and am eager to try them. A horn company you might want to check out is Lupifaro. I will be checking these horns out at the end of May and early June. Some of the new Selmer saxophones are great but you just have to find the model you like and try a couple of the same model.\nWhich TM model do you use? I am hoping to try the unlacquered or cognac lacquered with no high F#.\nI will contact Drew or Randy soon and see when I can try them out. Randy told me he wanted to sit down with me to describe the horn so I will keep you posted.\nThanks for the post William\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229287,	0),
(229289,	7793,	'William Bua',	'bua6969@comcast.net',	'',	'76.114.42.142',	'2013-05-16 08:23:08',	'2013-05-16 15:23:08',	'Hey Zack,\nI play a cognac lacquered horn with high F#.  I have owned 2 of them.  One was an early model and the one I have is one of the new ones with some slight improvements from the earlier model.  My original one was a M\'Lady and the new one is engraved TM custom. Randy made some small changes to tone hole size on high F and subtle changes to palm key height as well as improvements to both thumb rests.  Anyway, I can\'t wait to read your review.  The last time I attempted a review, I got booted off the sotw forum, so I am done with reviews, lol!!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229288,	0),
(229290,	7794,	'Dave Erato',	'david.erato@me.com',	'https://www.daviderato.com',	'72.135.234.73',	'2011-09-12 07:28:06',	'2011-09-12 14:28:06',	'Interesting interview with Jack Snavely.  Great find.  He\'s another iconic woodwind teacher, whose influence is still felt (in the Milwaukee area at least, 30 some years after he\'s retired to Florida).  There are still some nasty words about him scratched in the music locker room door.  Most likely he was a hard teacher.\nI know my teacher was a Snavely student for years.  Tongue in EE is one of the first thing he teaches, though later in lessons he does say open the throat.  Hmm.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229291,	7794,	'Jacob',	'Jakedeving@yahoo.com',	'',	'76.94.247.1',	'2011-09-12 09:22:12',	'2011-09-12 16:22:12',	'Years of lies and deceit! Pains me to know that more people don\'t know about this. Why don\'t they?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229292,	7794,	'Bret Pimentel',	'bpimentel+wordpress@gmail.com',	'https://bretpimentel.com/',	'209.147.246.138',	'2011-09-12 10:07:52',	'2011-09-12 17:07:52',	'I think the underlying concept here is what is referred to as \"<a href=\"https://bretpimentel.com/what-is-voicing/\" rel=\"nofollow ugc\">voicing</a>.\" There isn\'t just \"AH\" and \"EE,\" but a whole continuous range of voicings. We\'ve all got our own set of voicing problems to solve--some of us will solve them by opening up (\"AH\"), some will solve them by focusing in (\"EE\"), and most of us will need to apply each of those concepts at some point.\nIt\'s a particularly difficult issue on the saxophone. Clarinetists get their best results way at the \"EE\" end of the spectrum, while flutists, double reed players, and brass players tend to have success at the opposite end: \"AH\" or even \"OH.\" The saxophone requires hitting a sweet spot somewhere in the middle, so precision is a must. <a href=\"https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//improve-your-tone-without-picking-up-the-horn/#comment-318\" rel=\"nofollow ugc\">Mouthpiece pitch</a> exercises are great for developing this.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229293,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-12 20:17:50',	'2011-09-13 03:17:50',	'Yeah, seems like a very knowledgable player, your lucky to have soaked up some Joe Allard wisdom, even indirectly. Thanks Dave!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229290,	1),
(229294,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-12 20:19:09',	'2011-09-13 03:19:09',	'Deceit indeed! But seriously, I think it\'s just that people associate the \"AH\" sound with a big voice, like Pavarotti blaring out a dramatic final note. Anyhow, it\'s great to get some other perspectives.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229291,	1),
(229295,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-12 20:20:09',	'2011-09-13 03:20:09',	'Wow, you\'re taking this entire topic to a whole \'nuther level, I really never thought about it that way. That\'s why it\'s always cool to get the doubler\'s perspective. Thanks so much for this Bret!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229292,	1),
(229296,	7794,	'Bill Plake',	'bplake@earthlink.net',	'https://billplakemusic.org',	'99.96.5.117',	'2011-09-12 21:48:22',	'2011-09-13 04:48:22',	'I discovered those Joe Allard videos (I think they were called, \"The Master Speaks\") when they first came out in about 1989 or so. I was immensely helped by the idea of voicing the sound with the \"eee\" syllable. What that does in the simplest physical sense, is it changes the velocity of the air stream (speeding it up), which not only makes the sound more focused and powerful, but also, more easy and efficient to produce.\nHaving said that, it\'s important to realize that your tongue should, and will, move from this position as you play. Joe Allard referred to this as the \"internal embouchure\". I think it\'s a good idea to aim for the \"eee\" shape, but not try to hold it there rigidly. If you do Joe\'s \"playing the mouthpiece\" scale exercise, you\'ll find your tongue moving all over the place (but not your jaw!) That\'s the whole point.\nAnother thing to keep in mind with the position of the tongue is the soft palate (the soft tissue right behind the hard palate). In voicing anything, whether vocal or into a wind instrument, it is the internal shape of the mouth that largely conditions sound quality and projection, even intonation. That shape is determined by whatever the tongue and soft palate are doing together in any given moment.\nOne word of caution about this all however. If a saxophonist doesn\'t have good independence between what the tongue does and the jaw does there can be some serious problems if he or she tries to play with the \"eee\"shape. A simple way to test this is to whisper \"la la la\" a few times as you look into a mirror. If your jaw moves with your tongue, you\'ve lost that independence (it\'s easily regained through brief practice). If this independence isn\'t in place, there\'ll be lots of reed pinching.\nSorry for going on so long about this, and thanks again, Doron for a wonderful article. That bit of advice is going to help a lot of saxophonists, I think. (And what wonderful, insightful input you\'ve already had from your readers about it! I especially appreciate Bret Pimentel\'s highly useful advice.)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229297,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-12 21:59:08',	'2011-09-13 04:59:08',	'As usual, I\'ve learned so much for your insights. It looks like the comments here are truly taking this article to the next level. I\'m excited to take what you\'ve laid out here into the practice room to take my own playing to the next level.\nThanks Bill!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229296,	1),
(229298,	7794,	'Saxophone Lessons',	'datinglopa@yahoo.com',	'http://www.SaxophoneTeachers.com',	'119.30.35.83',	'2011-09-13 13:59:32',	'2011-09-13 19:59:32',	'Wow, you’re taking this entire topic to a whole ‘nuther level, I really never thought about it that way. Interesting interview',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229299,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-13 14:01:44',	'2011-09-13 21:01:44',	'Glad you\'re enjoying it!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229298,	1),
(229300,	7794,	'Jacob Mertz',	'mertzjd@gmail.com',	'',	'70.105.47.232',	'2011-09-13 19:59:13',	'2011-09-14 02:59:13',	'Although I would agree with Mr. Allard\'s discouragement of the \"AH\" position, I am not a proponent of the \"EE\" position either.\nI studied saxophone with Al Regni, who is an on-call saxophonist with the NY Phil.  He is a former Allard student, and he told me some of the oral cavity shapes he was taught by Allard made him gag.  Think about an exaggerated \"EE\" sound (almost like a hiss).  Personally, that makes ME gag!  With that being said, Mr. Regni always told me that he would come out of his lessons with Joe sounding like a million bucks.  He taught me using a \"OO\" syllable.  I like this (and teach this) because if needed I can bring my pitch up a little bit to an \"EE\" position, but I can also bring it down too and not lose focus.\nIn my opinion, a lot of the throat position depends upon type of music being played, as well.  This is a bit of a generalization, but classical saxophonists tend to play with a higher pitch center (colder, faster air) than jazz saxophonists.  There are two theses that can be found online dealing with physical differences between different types of saxophonists, one by Vanessa Hasbrook and one by Joel Patrick Vanderheyden.  I know Allard wasn\'t a classical saxophone teacher per se, but maybe he felt that the \"EE\" oral cavity with a good starting base when he taught.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229301,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-13 21:37:15',	'2011-09-14 04:37:15',	'What you\'re saying makes a lot of sense. I really like what Bret had to share about working with a variety of voicings. When it comes to music there\'s very rarely a one-size-fits all solution, so I\'m glad that the commenters here are giving a broader perspective on the whole \"open throat\" concept.\nThat said, I do agree that \"EE\" is a good place to start, as it\'s really helped me quite a bit in the short amount of time that I\'ve been playing that way.\nThanks Jacob!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229300,	1),
(229302,	7794,	'Jason The Obscure',	'BFlat.harmonic@gmail.com',	'',	'99.27.134.117',	'2011-09-16 19:19:48',	'2011-09-17 02:19:48',	'Bret Pimental for pres.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229303,	7794,	'Bret Pimentel',	'bpimentel+wordpress@gmail.com',	'https://bretpimentel.com/',	'209.147.242.202',	'2011-09-16 19:29:25',	'2011-09-17 02:29:25',	'Thanks for your support, but I have retired from politics to spend more time with my reed knife.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229302,	0),
(229304,	7794,	'Doug Delaney',	'douglasdelaney@comcasts.net',	'',	'67.174.193.33',	'2011-09-25 13:11:03',	'2011-09-25 20:11:03',	'After all these years my thoughts are confirmed. Open your throat! Impossible-raise your tongue, lower you tongue, that\'s all you can do. Thanks, Doron and Phil Sobel.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229305,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-09-25 17:41:38',	'2011-09-26 00:41:38',	'Yep. It\'s also about assuming the same vocal position playing the note as you would if you were *singing* the note - so no bizarre contortions required.\nAnyhow, thanks for stopping in, I\'m glad you found the article interesting!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229304,	1),
(229306,	7794,	'TenorMoxie',	'geophone@hotmail.com',	'',	'75.111.57.106',	'2011-11-06 11:04:52',	'2011-11-06 19:04:52',	'Jim Romaine of Drake University has some interesting ideas on voicing.  His approach is based on use of the position of the larynx (Adam\'s apple) for voicing, especially in the altissimo range.  It\'s not the difference between \"aaah\" and \"eeeh\" it\'s the difference between singing high notes and low notes.  He has demos of the effect of larynx position on Youtube.  I think the source of confusion about the tongue position is that the tendency is to move the tongue when the larynx is moved without the feedback that one gets when actually singing.  It takes a little conscious practice to move the larynx without moving the tongue while playing.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229307,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-11-06 12:27:54',	'2011-11-06 20:27:54',	'I agree, it\'s not quite as simple as pronouncing \"EEE,\" I simply think it\'s a step in the right direction. You bring up some great points about not moving the tongue, as that\'s one of the subtler aspects of tone production. Thanks for chiming in!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229306,	1),
(229308,	7794,	'Haywood',	'hgiles@gmail.com',	'',	'50.73.227.77',	'2011-11-22 12:17:33',	'2011-11-22 20:17:33',	'A-E-I-O-U...all have their place in tone production, I\'d imagine.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229309,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-11-22 12:33:16',	'2011-11-22 20:33:16',	'Absolutely, and each vowel sound can create its own sound effect, but for the most open sound on the saxophone, I think that EEE is a good starting place. If you haven\'t done so, I\'d check out Bill Plake and Bret Pimentel\'s comments as well as the others above for some more insights.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229308,	1),
(229310,	7794,	'Tobias Haecker',	'mail@saxophonistisches.de',	'http://saxophonistisches.de/',	'91.12.190.171',	'2011-12-27 14:06:20',	'2011-12-27 20:06:20',	'Liebman was also one of Allards pupils and in his book your personal saxophone sound shall be very influenced by allard.\nI got one of the rare copies of allards VHS in my hands and I put them on youtube.\nhttps://saxophonistisches.de/der-grose-blattschraubenreport/\nI would say, he says a lot of interessting things, but other things are more a personal view and others are just outdated.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229311,	7794,	'Tobias Haecker',	'mail@saxophonistisches.de',	'http://saxophonistisches.de/',	'91.12.190.171',	'2011-12-27 14:08:26',	'2011-12-27 20:08:26',	'oh, wrong link:\nhttps://saxophonistisches.de/joe-allard-the-master-speaks/',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229310,	0),
(229312,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2011-12-27 22:25:15',	'2011-12-28 06:25:15',	'Yes, Liebman\'s book is great! As for the videos, those are really cool as well, however, I\'ve heard that they do not represent Allard at his best, since he was starting to go into his Alzheimer\'s disease by the time those videos we made.\nThanks Tobias!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229311,	1),
(229313,	7794,	'Matheus',	'sparks.220@hotmail.com',	'',	'187.35.191.202',	'2012-01-25 02:50:10',	'2012-01-25 10:50:10',	'Guy... thank you. I am a singer and one of the things that I have been studying actually is that we are supposed to work on developing an \"open throat\" to sing. If we don\'t do it we will supposed to sound weak and thin, and strained too. I didn\'t believe too much about that, simply because it didn\'t make too much sense to me and trying to sing like that was no something natural to me. I\'ve been studying things from various vocal coaches and hardly I found something that really convinced me. Then I found your this page and WOW, now yes, convinced me. There are lots of misunderstandings about lots of things, in singing too: \"singing with a high larynx is bad and bla bla bla, you will sound forced if you do it and bla bla bla...\" The position of the larynx while singing was one of the myths I luckily could discover the truth. Well... thanks for showing us that. I don\'t play saxophone but you made me a better singer, thank you ^^.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229314,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-01-25 10:12:13',	'2012-01-25 18:12:13',	'Wow, that\'s very cool that you\'ve found this article helpful! I would imagine that a powerful and focused airstream is a good thing to have whether you play sax or sing, so I can see the similarity.\nI am curious how you came across this site, however(?).\nAt any rate, thanks so much for chiming in!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229313,	1),
(229315,	7794,	'Matheus',	'sparks.220@hotmail.com',	'',	'200.148.49.185',	'2012-01-25 20:37:10',	'2012-01-26 04:37:10',	'I was doing one of my Google researches and then I  found your site. I started to read and then when you \'said\' that the throat cannot move I felt like \'BINGO\'! THAT makes sense, and when you do exercises to get rid of tension in your tongue, neck, jaw or even in the muscles near the throat you feel like you have an \'open throat\' - not because it was closed but yes because you were under bad tension (yes, there\'s a good tension; a good tension that you put on your vocal folds to change the way your voice sounds. If well done it\'ll not damage them.), what can hamper your singing and even when you play an instrument where a \'free\' throat is need, like a saxophone. Thanks again.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229314,	0),
(229316,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-01-25 20:45:48',	'2012-01-26 04:45:48',	'A \"free throat\" - that\'s a good way of thinking about it!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229315,	1),
(229317,	7794,	'Matheus',	'sparks.220@hotmail.com',	'',	'200.148.49.185',	'2012-01-26 07:22:19',	'2012-01-26 15:22:19',	'A \"free throat\" - sure!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229316,	0),
(229318,	7794,	'jay weinstein',	'jw.hf@live.com',	'',	'63.226.194.78',	'2012-11-11 18:35:26',	'2012-11-12 02:35:26',	'hey guys\njay weinstein here   the guy who write The master Speaks...\nthis the first post i have ever written since i wrote the book in 1988   so take heed  it may be the last\ni find some of the posts pretty funny as a pro player for any years and hanging with guys like M Brecker. way back  who loved the book\nI was the who put in print the EEE tongue placement via joe\'s instruction\nforget al regnis ooo stuff in fact the ee works so well this is the only real way..   in fact one new tip is at hand to even put the  farthest  back, sides of the tongue resting on the upper teeth so that ( without the mouthpiece in your mouth), your tongue is almost out of your mouth!  the back of he tonge is moved forward so that it sits up very high arched in your mouth\nyou are making the cavity smaller, where th mouthpiece fits into,  in your mouth giving you even more pitch control  and volume power\nyou can email me at jw.hf@livel.com if you have a quetion',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229319,	7794,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.52.64',	'2012-11-11 18:55:13',	'2012-11-12 02:55:13',	'Hello Jay,\nVery cool to have you chime in here, and it\'s very generous of you to offer you email for people with questions. I\'m glad to see that we\'re pretty much on the same page in terms of the \"EEE\" tongue position.\nSo when you say, \"in fact one new tip is at hand to even put the farthest back, sides of the tongue resting on the upper teeth so that ( without the mouthpiece in your mouth), your tongue is almost out of your mouth! the back of he tonge is moved forward so that it sits up very high arched in your mouth\"...\nDo you mean to say that this is good advice, or is it one of the \"funny\" tips, as in funny = incorrect, or is this something that you actually recommend?\nDoron',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229318,	1),
(229320,	7794,	'Saxophone Lessons',	'reyad.hasan86@gmail.com',	'http://saxophonehub.com/saxophone-lessons',	'180.234.54.12',	'2013-09-29 00:14:00',	'2013-09-29 06:14:00',	'Really useful and informative tropic.I am a sax fan. Love to play music. Thanks for sharing such a good post.Thanks again.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
