(229343,	7796,	'Doron Orenstein',	'doron@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'https://www.bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com//',	'76.168.48.206',	'2013-04-25 15:05:43',	'2013-04-25 22:05:43',	'My pleasure! (spelled \"Doron\", btw). :)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229342,	1),
(229344,	7796,	'Albert Mensinga',	'info@albertmensingacreative.nl',	'https://albertmensingacreative.nl/muziek/',	'77.161.153.58',	'2016-07-13 01:20:08',	'2016-07-13 08:20:08',	'Great stuff here. And indeed: Stitt was one of the few classic masters and major influences on both alto and tenor. Another superb player who even added the soprano to the range (Sound Pieces, the shadow of your smile!!!) is Oliver Nelson. I find modern players (post 80s) to have a sound an setup that prefers allroundness and easiness (of playing) above personal sound. There are exceptions, but not too many.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229345,	7797,	'Brian Brody',	'brian@brianmitchellbrody.com',	'',	'68.12.101.42',	'2013-04-26 12:57:36',	'2013-04-26 18:57:36',	'Thank you for this review.\nI am floored by the immense dark sound the Yani is giving you.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229346,	7797,	'Paul Hrycaj',	'plhrycaj@yahoo.com',	'https://www.alljazzsax.blogspot.com',	'130.39.60.39',	'2013-04-26 15:13:59',	'2013-04-26 22:13:59',	'This is very helpful; thanks for these efforts.  For a clearer comparison of the instruments, though, hearing the same music played might be useful.  Like maybe a slow passage or melody and then a fast one, but the same passages for all the instruments.  Just a thought for future comparisons.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229347,	7797,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'99.156.125.176',	'2013-04-26 16:55:00',	'2013-04-26 23:55:00',	'Hey Paul,\nGreat Advice. I am going to try and make some videos of me test playing saxophones, mouthpieces, and ligatures and include a piece of music or etude while demoing each piece of equipment.\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229348,	7797,	'Paul Hrycaj',	'plhrycaj@yahoo.com',	'',	'64.134.24.124',	'2013-04-28 10:42:26',	'2013-04-28 17:42:26',	'Sounds good, Zach.  With regard to this idea of demonstrating a piece of equipment using the same music, here\'s a video you might enjoy, if you haven\'t seen it before; James Galway demonstrating a bunch of flutes.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G0n3n3N3SOY\n--Paul',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229349,	7797,	'TenorMoxie',	'geophone@hotmail.com',	'',	'75.111.3.29',	'2013-05-02 14:23:29',	'2013-05-02 21:23:29',	'The bronze Yani is a solid phosphor bronze body, isn\'t it?  The second paragraph refers to a bronze finish.  Great sound from that thing.\nHave you considered comparing bronze vs. brass versions of the same model Yani?  That could be quite interesting.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229350,	7797,	'jas.huggins',	'jameshuggins99@yahoo.com',	'',	'173.216.169.41',	'2013-05-02 15:05:57',	'2013-05-02 22:05:57',	'hello, please tell me about the walstein Bauhaus tenor saxophone made in china.i hear they are some real good horns.i,am thinking about buying one.they are some nice blowing horns.made of bronze body.bronze saxophone sound better then brass.i been playing pro. for 49 years.and this horn sound almost like the yangisawa 992 tenor.and the price is great too.please try one for me.in the u.s.a.contact russ becker at russ becker.com.this guy is great to talk with to.oh yes with the oct.key was design let the yangisawa tenor.from.jas.huggins',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229351,	7797,	'TenorMoxie',	'geophone@hotmail.com',	'',	'75.111.3.29',	'2013-05-03 11:20:23',	'2013-05-03 18:20:23',	'Spam Flag',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229350,	0),
(229352,	7797,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'99.156.125.176',	'2013-05-07 15:38:56',	'2013-05-07 22:38:56',	'I have considered this. I own a yanagisawa 991 tenor and it was fun to compare it to the 992. I actually prefer the 992 over my 991 but would most likely buy the bronze neck and get some work down on my upper stack before considering selling it and purchasing a new horn.\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229349,	0),
(229353,	7797,	'George W',	'gwidener7@gmail.com',	'',	'71.163.178.169',	'2013-11-30 00:41:39',	'2013-11-30 08:41:39',	'I am amazed by how dumpy the Selmer sounds, and how much the Yani sounds like a vintage Selmer combined with the velvet of a Keilwerth.  The Selmers are so rough these days.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229354,	7797,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'99.156.125.176',	'2013-11-30 08:19:00',	'2013-11-30 16:19:00',	'Hey George,\nI really like the Yanagisawa\'s and vintage Selmer\'s. I still believe that Selmer make\'s a great modern horn but not as good as some of the vintage mark VI\'s. Of all the Selmer models (series II, series III, and reference 54), I would have to say the Reference 54 is the best modern model from Selmer (at least that is what I think). I would have to say that with the modern Selmer\'s, you have to find a good one. I believe they are consistent from horn to horn but not as consistent as a Yanagisawa or Yamaha. On the other-hand, when you find a really good Mark VI or reference 54, it is hard to put down.\nWhat is your main setup George?\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229353,	0),
(229355,	7797,	'Larry Weintraub',	'Lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.147.121',	'2015-09-16 09:06:11',	'2015-09-16 16:06:11',	'Zach: I thought the Yani had a darker sound than the Ref 54. But both played well. I guess it depends on how it feels and sounds to you, the player.\nI thought the Ref 54 alto had a nice sound too.\nHave you ever played and /or posted anything about the Cannonball Big Bell Raven Saxophones. A lot of people including me like these horns better than the new Selmers, Keilwerths, Yamahas etc. For 1 thing they are a lot less money. Secondly they play extremely well with really good intonation. To me my Cannonball tenor is almost like a Conn 10M in sound and response. There is less resistance on the lowest notes because the Big Bell creates less turbulence  according to Cannonball. Also there necks which are a special design really help out in the sound and resisitance area. A lot of players are using these horns now instead of their Selmer Mk Vi\'s and they sound great on the Cannonballs. They are also more sturdy than any horn I\'ve seen. The low Bb, B and C are double braced which helps in keeping the horn in regulation.\nIf you go to their website here is what they say. Yes the horns are made in Tawain by a really good shop. The key is that they are then sent to Cannonball\'s shop in Salt Lake City where they horn are adjusted and made to Cannonballs specifications. hey have 1 guy who does sopranos and altos, another guy just works on the tenors and yet another guy just works on the baris. Also the necks are shaped and made to Cannonballs specs. So basically they get a good horn from Taiwan and then change it and shape it into a great horn.  You ought to contact the owner Tevis and check these horns out.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229356,	7797,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'206.169.91.210',	'2015-09-18 09:19:15',	'2015-09-18 16:19:15',	'Hello Larry,\nI have played the Cannonball line of saxophones at various music stores and trade shows but have never sat down with one over a couple weeks. My favorite model is the Key Artist Series. I have seen new saxophones coming out every year and have to say that the manufacturing and quality control in Taiwan has gotten better and better. Having the saxophone setup after it arrives to the U.S. from Taiwan by a trained technician makes a big difference in play-ability. I am seeing more and more music stores offering their own line of saxophones and their setup is what differentiates there line from the other Taiwanese manufactured saxophones. The Cannonball saxophones are priced very competitively  and can be found at many are almost all Sam Ash music stores. Some other brands I would consider as well would be Viking, MacSax, P Mauriat, R.S. Berkeley, Lupifaro, and Ishimori. If you have not had a chance and get to, Ishimori makes one of the best \"vintage\" saxophones I have test played in a long time and hope to stop by their booth at NAMM if they attend this year.\nZach\n954-292-2951\nThanks for the Feedback,\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229357,	7798,	'Dave Thomasson',	'saxjazzed@gmail.com',	'',	'173.58.226.93',	'2013-11-23 12:01:36',	'2013-11-23 20:01:36',	'\"Last week, I received an alto and tenor of the M21 model with the nickel silver bell and neck on both. The tenor has the white rollers and the super 20 guards which is pretty cool. I also own the m58 alto and one of the original m40 tenors with a set up from Tenor Madness. I can confidently say that the models have distinctly different tonal colors which gives me great satisfaction. The m21 tenor has a deeper and more direct presence than the m40 which has a sweeter vibe. The m21 alto is actually darker in tone than the m58 alto and reminds me of the solid silver Yanis that I have played in the past. Intonation is absolutely superb on the alto which again compares to the yani. I am equally as satisfied with the intonation on the tenor. The word \"slot\" comes to mind when playing the tenor as in \"The intervals slot really well\" meaning the intonation is very, very solid and accurate between the intervals. Lastly, I simply feel good playing these horns from tone to action and they look really hip.\"',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229358,	7800,	'John Laughter',	'JSAXL@aol.com',	'',	'68.109.33.148',	'2015-05-12 19:18:29',	'2015-05-13 01:18:29',	'I own two 67 RGL altos and really enjoy them. I have listened to the Youtube clip. Excellent but I can\'t find any online examples of the Osby Signature alto really being pushed for some serious volume and a brighter tone. Do we know if it has the ability to go in that direction as well with the right mouthpiece? Thanks.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229359,	7801,	'Joe Q.',	'jwosnick@gmail.com',	'',	'13.12.254.95',	'2014-01-14 08:58:20',	'2014-01-14 16:58:20',	'Bari players have a trick for transposing bass clef music written at concert pitch -- \"imagine\" the music written in treble clef, adjust keys and accidentals appropriately, and you\'re golden. Useful for playing cello music on the bari.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229360,	7801,	'Larry Weintraub',	'Lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.43.183',	'2014-02-23 15:44:09',	'2014-02-23 23:44:09',	'Actually the alto part should be transposed up a major 6th from the concert pitch. Writing the notes a minor 3rd below concert pitch put the alto in the wrong octave.\nWith tenor you have to remember that the transposition is actually a major 9th above concert pitch. However if you are reading out of a fake book you can just read up a major 2nd like clarinet, soprano and tpt. Just realize that the tenor will sound an octave lower than those instruments if they are playing the same written note on the staff paper.\nIn college we played string quartets and transposed them as we played them. As a tenor player I had the viola part. So this is what I did. I read the alto clef part in bass clef but I adjusted the octave. So if the note to play was C, 3rd space alto clef. In bass clef is a D which is the pitch I need. To adjust for the correct octave just play the D as a 4th line D treble clef.\nI hope this helps.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229361,	7801,	'johnny argent',	'johnargent@earthlink.net',	'',	'75.84.252.25',	'2014-03-18 23:44:42',	'2014-03-19 06:44:42',	'One tip for guys who want to jam with or work in Blues/Rock, or Funk bands where reading is not part of the landscape? Be ready to play fluently in E and A, which, of course puts you in F# or B if you\'re a tenor player. Way, WAY back in the day, when horn players were generally the band leaders, lots of tunes were in horn-friendly keys - I know: we should be proficient in ALL keys, BUT with the advent of guitar players dominating the band scene, they like their \"native\" or low, open string keys like E and A. I heard Robert Plant (Led Zep) say that part of his high vocal approach was a result of Jimmy Page wanting things in E.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229362,	7801,	'Keith Charles Edwards',	'keithsy@yahoo.com',	'',	'71.125.192.37',	'2014-04-28 14:45:36',	'2014-04-28 21:45:36',	'I must do so. I can do it. I know I can. I just have to put my mind to it.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229363,	7801,	'Dr David Mercer',	'doctordavidtutoring@gmail.com',	'https://www.doctordavidtutoring.com.au',	'114.72.124.12',	'2016-11-18 23:54:14',	'2016-11-19 07:54:14',	'if the key is A then for my alto sax I play in F sharp key?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229364,	7801,	'Smitty',	'Greatsax4u@gmail.com',	'',	'69.136.25.68',	'2018-06-22 08:33:50',	'2018-06-22 14:33:50',	'I was told by my fellow musician\'s that play guitar that the song they were playing was in A minor. So I play a Alto Sax and I was trying to find that transposed Key that I would explain what key I needed to be in. I got  lost!!! lol  Any suggestions??  Shoot me an email if you have the answer..I could use it. :)',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229365,	7801,	'Joe Q.',	'jwosnick@gmail.com',	'',	'199.212.27.189',	'2018-06-22 08:42:29',	'2018-06-22 14:42:29',	'For key transposition, you go down by a minor third (three semitones). Concert A minor becomes played F# minor on the alto.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229364,	0),
(229366,	7802,	'scott silbert',	'zootfan@aol.com',	'',	'68.49.106.119',	'2014-03-13 17:19:38',	'2014-03-14 00:19:38',	'Great list, but I would also have included Keith Bradbury (mojobari). He has done some stunning work for me over the past few years.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229367,	7802,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'166.137.208.36',	'2014-03-13 20:44:52',	'2014-03-14 03:44:52',	'Hey Scott,\nThank you for the add on. Please feel free Scott to add any info in your comment about Keith Bradbury. I would like to grow this list with more names so players have more options for guys to check out. Thanks for the suggestion.\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229366,	0),
(229368,	7802,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'166.137.208.36',	'2014-03-13 20:45:44',	'2014-03-14 03:45:44',	'Hey Scott,\nThank you for the add on. Please feel free Scott to add any info in your comment about Keith Bradbury. I would like to grow this list with more names so players have more options for guys to check out. Thanks for the suggestion.\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229366,	0),
(229369,	7802,	'Alonzo Wright',	'myalonzo@gmail.com',	'',	'67.243.34.92',	'2014-03-13 21:17:30',	'2014-03-14 04:17:30',	'You should also include Norbert Stachel. He is a great player and mouthpiece tech. He recently did some great work on my soprano and tenor pieces. He should be number ten for sure.  Check him out  https://www.norbertstachel.com/#top',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229370,	7802,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zach@bestsaxophonewebsiteever.com',	'',	'166.137.208.34',	'2014-03-13 22:23:28',	'2014-03-14 05:23:28',	'Hey Alonzo,\nIt\'s funny you mention Norbert because I just heard a bit about him from a couple players. Will definitely check him out.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229369,	0),
(229371,	7802,	'Ryan',	'ochsryan@hotmail.com',	'',	'159.105.215.69',	'2014-03-14 10:49:11',	'2014-03-14 17:49:11',	'I would second the Keith Bradbury recommendation.  He has done great work for me.  His youtube (mojobari) videos are extremely informative.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/user/MojoBari',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229372,	7802,	'Larry Weintraub',	'Lweintraub1@cox.net',	'',	'70.160.43.183',	'2014-04-04 19:55:35',	'2014-04-05 02:55:35',	'Another guy who does really good work is John Reilly of Hollywood, CA. He\'s kind of a low key, word of mouth kind of guy. He seems to be the go to guy on the West Coast Coast. Both Dan Higgins the Hollywood Studio guy and Tim Price the teacher/educator/player from Reading, PA both recommended him to me. He did some good work for me on a basic stock Babbitt HR current Link. He was also helpful in providing some good information to me on mpcs for my Masters Project in Music Ed.\nAnother guy who is very knowledgeable is Phil Barone of Brooklyn, NY. He has a great reputation although I have never played his mpcs or seen them in person. Guys like Lou Marini (Blues Brothers) play his mpcs and like them.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229373,	7802,	'Douglas Harlow',	'dhpsax@gmail.com',	'',	'50.151.158.209',	'2014-04-06 05:07:38',	'2014-04-06 12:07:38',	'Phil Engleman (Phil-tone) is a master. I play a customized Equinox (slightly darker) on my tenor &amp; a Meridian on my alto. I am totally enamoured with the Meridian. It brought out the sound in me I have been seeking for literally 35 years!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229374,	7802,	'GIlberto',	'gilbertpz@gmail.com',	'',	'201.243.176.51',	'2014-05-22 05:24:06',	'2014-05-22 12:24:06',	'Great article, thanks for sharing the true important which is the men behind the sound and their vision of perfection.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229375,	7802,	'Federico',	'hulszsax@gmail.com',	'',	'189.203.252.141',	'2014-06-12 12:28:14',	'2014-06-12 19:28:14',	'I also think very highly of Phil-Tone. I have a custom hard rubber Link, which was my main piece for almost four years, until I recently bought a Sapphire (also by Phil-tone), which has become my main piece now, I immediately felt right at home with it and it allows me to produce to sound I\'m looking for. Very  high level of craftsmanship. Very recommended.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229373,	0),
(229376,	7802,	'Alessio',	'alessio.roman86@facebook.com',	'',	'87.0.235.133',	'2014-06-12 13:18:57',	'2014-06-12 20:18:57',	'You miss Aaron Drake, a real magician!\nhttps://www.drakemouthpieces.com/',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229377,	7802,	'nikolaj',	'nikolajnikitin@gmail.com',	'https://nikolajnikitin.com',	'195.91.11.171',	'2014-06-12 14:11:47',	'2014-06-12 21:11:47',	'one more master: freddie gregory',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229378,	7802,	'Norbert Stachel',	'nstachel1@gmail.com',	'https://www.norbertstachel.com',	'66.249.83.174',	'2015-08-28 00:26:42',	'2015-08-28 07:26:42',	'Hi Zak, I\'m actually born and raised from the San Francisco Bay Area. I am the mouthpiece refacing teacher that taught one of the guys towards the top of your list! I\'ve been refacing for 20 years. Mouthpieces that I\'ve refaced are currently being played by all 3 saxophonists in she band Tower Of Power, Wayne Escoffery, Ian Hendrickson-Smith in the Jimmy Fallon Tonight Show band with The Roots, Nelson Rangel, Justo Armario, Steve Slagle, &amp; hundreds of pro players all over the planet. In addition to the aforementioned, you can also hear recordings of my refaced mouthpieces on all of the saxes and clarinets on my new release: Norbert Stachel - Shades Of The Bay. Cheers!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229379,	7802,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'206.169.91.210',	'2015-08-28 11:40:15',	'2015-08-28 18:40:15',	'Hello Norbert,\nThat\'s great to hear. I am always looking for members and readers to comment with mouthpiece re-facers whom they enjoy using. There are more and more people getting into this business so its hard for me to keep track. Congrat\'s on the new c.d. I might be doing a 2nd  article on mouthpiece re-facing but not sure at the moment.\nZach\n954-292-2951',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229380,	7802,	'Jeff Littlefield',	'jefflitlefield@yahoo.com',	'',	'69.42.1.80',	'2015-09-09 10:38:20',	'2015-09-09 16:38:20',	'I was gona mention Norbert Satchel, but the wow there ya go......saxman Joe Call told me about him.  Great sit Zach, Well I gotta to talk to Norbert about a mouthpiece',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229381,	7802,	'Jeff Littlefield',	'jefflitlefield@yahoo.com',	'',	'69.42.1.80',	'2015-09-09 10:44:14',	'2015-09-09 16:44:14',	'I have to proof read my posts better, great site Zach and saxman Joe Calo, That\'s better, Jeff.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229382,	7802,	'Mark Taylor',	'mark@taylormademusic.com.au',	'https://marktaylor.com.au',	'121.209.235.115',	'2016-05-31 06:04:52',	'2016-05-31 12:04:52',	'Great article Zach, thanks!\nAlso great to read the comments with those additional names mentioned.\nI concur about John Reilly in Los Angeles.\nHe has done some excellent refacing and customising for me on several pieces.\nJohn has a website: https://www.jrmpcdr.com/\nAlso in LA, studio woodwind player John Yoakum is highly respected for his mouthpiece refacing work.\nAnother guy in New York who comes extremely highly recommended and is very highly regarded by many of the top Broadway, studio and jazz players there is Scott Shachter.\n[Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]\nAmongst many great players, he was working on some of Michael Brecker\'s mouthpieces prior to Mike\'s illness and passing.\nHere in Australia, Gabriel Vatavu is outstanding!.\ngabrielvatavurepairs.com/mouthpiece.php',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229383,	7802,	'J. Dennis',	'j.dennisarceo@gmail.com',	'https://www.jdennisarceo.com',	'112.205.135.1',	'2016-06-05 11:19:12',	'2016-06-05 18:19:12',	'It was actually surprising not to see Jennifer Price on the list. She, as not too many people know, was actually the brians behind Guardalla mouthpieces. The very famous and well imitated sound of Michael Brecker\'s was produced using a Guardalla piece. She now has her own line of mouthpieces called Vigilante.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229384,	7802,	'Zachary M Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'107.201.109.91',	'2016-06-05 12:59:49',	'2016-06-05 19:59:49',	'Hello J Dennis,\nThanks for the update. This is a working list and there have been many more mouthpiece re-facers hitting the scene that do deserve credit as well. I have heard great things about Jennifer\'s work but have not had the chance to try one of her mouthpieces yet.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229385,	7802,	'Lalo Rojas',	'lalorojassax@gmail.com',	'',	'190.53.69.187',	'2016-06-05 22:37:54',	'2016-06-06 04:37:54',	'These are some more names to add to this great list...\nFred Lamberson\nJohannes Gerber\nJoe Giardullo\nJohn Thomas',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229386,	7802,	'Brian Brody',	'Saxophoneforhire@gmail.com',	'',	'208.54.38.152',	'2016-06-06 04:48:03',	'2016-06-06 10:48:03',	'Greg Wier is also a very good mouthpiece maker and refacer  to add. An accomplished flute and saxophonist. He is based in St. Augustine, Florida.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229387,	7802,	'Toni Linder',	'toni.linder@me.com',	'',	'178.196.239.90',	'2016-08-12 02:57:17',	'2016-08-12 09:57:17',	'I think you forgot my man Ron Pablo Coelho of RPC mouthpieces!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229388,	7802,	'Scott Meier',	'meier.scott@gmail.com',	'',	'66.211.206.7',	'2016-10-03 11:22:58',	'2016-10-03 18:22:58',	'I just thought I would add the amazing work of Johannes Gerber.  The piece he made for me represents the cleanest craftsmanship I have owned.  I should mention that I have 50+ mouthpieces by famous makers currently in my collection .  Also, I have a bari piece by Ron Coelho that is unparalleled in its playability.  I have five A\'s without effort on my bari when I play this piece, and it has an amazing fat tone with an  extra wide dynamic range.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229389,	7802,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'107.201.109.91',	'2016-10-03 17:30:47',	'2016-10-04 00:30:47',	'Hello Scott,\nThanks for the add. I am looking to check out Johannes Gerber\'s mouthpieces soon. I always like to hear from other musicians about other great mouthpiece refacers.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229388,	0),
(229390,	7802,	'Joseph Delossantos',	'jdelossantos9six@gmail.com',	'',	'158.222.175.206',	'2017-01-17 17:02:42',	'2017-01-18 01:02:42',	'Add Matt Marantz to the list!! He refaced my jody jazz HR 7M and the tone on it feels great!!!!',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229391,	7802,	'David Suleiman',	'dsuleiman@berklee.edu',	'',	'76.24.8.252',	'2018-02-07 02:07:50',	'2018-02-07 08:07:50',	'Francois Louis is a very underrated mouthpiece Craftsman. I always get the same \"I didn\'t know he also made mpcs\" referring to his ligature work',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229392,	7802,	'Grant King Koeller',	'gkoeller@woh.rr.com',	'',	'104.231.2.120',	'2018-04-03 19:13:36',	'2018-04-04 01:13:36',	'His name is Brian. not Brain .....',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229393,	7802,	'Zach Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'107.201.109.91',	'2018-04-03 19:48:23',	'2018-04-04 01:48:23',	'Hello Grant,\nFixed. Thanks for catching that!\nBest,\nZach',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229392,	1),
(229394,	7802,	'Crispin Cioe',	'crispin@crispinmusic.com',	'http://www.crispinmusic.com',	'68.197.79.114',	'2019-05-31 20:14:23',	'2019-06-01 01:14:23',	'Norbert Stachel is brilliant, as a player and working on pieces.  He worked on my main tenor and alto mouthpieces (both vintage Guardala), as well as on a couple of other backup tenor pieces, and made them all infinitely more playable and enjoyable.  I would recommend Norbert to anyone.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229369,	0),
(229395,	7802,	'Memory of Music',	'pencho.minchev@abv.bg',	'',	'213.226.17.10',	'2019-09-18 01:58:10',	'2019-09-18 06:58:10',	'Hello everybody ! How about a good refacers i Europe. Do you know any ?!?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229396,	7802,	'David Hayes',	'davidhayes58@msn.com',	'',	'76.29.197.206',	'2019-12-13 13:29:07',	'2019-12-13 19:29:07',	'Hey Zach,\nGreat line up of mouthpiece makers and refacers. All of the add on\'s are also top notch people with great products. I was surprised that Jody Espina from JodyJazz mouthpieces wasn\'t mentioned. He\'s got one of the best selling mouthpieces the world. Just a thought...',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229397,	7802,	'Zachary Sollitto',	'zsollitto@bellsouth.net',	'',	'192.161.159.18',	'2019-12-13 15:11:15',	'2019-12-13 21:11:15',	'Hey David,\nGood point. I will be working on another updated article in the near future since there have been some new re-facers that have entered the market. I did not include Jody at the time because I was focused mainly on just re-facers and not the combination of mouthpieces and refacers at that time. The good news is we have so many options to choose from today compared to years past.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	229396,	0),
(229398,	7803,	'Steve Scheinberg',	'steve@zoominternet.net',	'',	'24.112.154.213',	'2015-03-21 11:59:33',	'2015-03-21 18:59:33',	'This tool is everything it claims to be. Trust me -- I\'ve been playing sax since Lyndon Johnson was President, and have tried everything to work on \"bad\" reeds. Maybe I just wasn\'t that good at it. Whatever. I\'ve just had almost no luck, and settled in to the habit of just tossing reeds that didn\'t play out of the box, then tossing good reeds that went \"bad.\"\nHowever, I\'m proud to say that, following the easy instructions in the Reed Geek videos, I recently was able to successfully balance a reed that I would have tossed away in the past. In fact, it became a very good reed. Again, this is something I have never been able to do before! It\'s all a matter of having the right tool and following the simple instructions. The key is to adjust very gently until you get a feel for roughly how much cane has to come off to make a difference.\nEven if you use the Reed Geek for nothing other than flattening the reed out of the box -- and periodically re-flattening as you use it -- it\'s worth it. You will get more usable reeds by doing nothing more than flattening -- even more by adjusting.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229399,	7803,	'Ben Proctor',	'benproctor530@gmail.com',	'https://reedbalancer.com/products',	'97.75.177.149',	'2016-10-25 14:30:02',	'2016-10-25 21:30:02',	'I was glad to hear that you had success with the brand of reed that worked well for you. I have had a lot of problems find one that worked well for me as well as lasted. I am grateful to also read some other techniques that will help with sound and playing. I will be sure to check this out. Do you have any other advice on the reed or techniques that could help?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229400,	7804,	'Dan moreno',	'morenosax@hotmail.com',	'',	'99.61.165.232',	'2015-05-01 06:05:44',	'2015-05-01 13:05:44',	'Thanks for yoour tips . I\'ll be visiting it  often to post comments on how I\'m doing with the intonation and altissimo notes.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229401,	7804,	'James Brown',	'bjgusr@gmail.com',	'',	'82.22.196.5',	'2015-10-03 13:25:31',	'2015-10-03 20:25:31',	'Hi Johnny,\nJust dropping by to say thanks for creating this altissimo course,  i had only been playing the sax for around 6 months when i started it. Within a couple of months i was managing to start to hit some of these elusive notes!\nAnyone else out there - i can assure you Johnny\'s course does work.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229402,	7804,	'Johnny Ferreira',	'johnny@howtoplaysaxophone.org',	'https://howtoplaysaxophone.org',	'24.86.112.202',	'2015-10-03 15:03:37',	'2015-10-03 22:03:37',	'well that\'s pretty good if you\'ve only been playing a few months. the exercises in Part 1 will also help to improve your overall tone in general so keep up with those daily if you can.\ncheers\nJohnny',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229403,	7804,	'harold pizer',	'haroldpizer@yahoo.com',	'https://haroldpizer@yahoo.com',	'32.214.170.80',	'2016-04-09 13:12:18',	'2016-04-09 20:12:18',	'Hi Johnny, very informative website. I was wondering how you felt about using alternate fingerings and overtones in order to bypass playing the left hand palm keys, which on my sax, a 1937 Martin Lion&amp;Crown tenor, are very difficult to play cleanly and precisely.\n    Here\'s what I do:  If I play G I can produce highD\n                                     If I play Ab I can produce high D#\n                                     If I play A I can produce high E\n                                                    Bb will produce high F\n                                                    B will produce high F#.  I tried getting high G by playing C but I havn\'t  been able to do that yet. I was just wondering if you have ever tried using this method?',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229404,	7804,	'Johnny',	'saxjohnny@johnnyferreira.com',	'https://howtoplaysaxophone.org',	'24.86.112.202',	'2016-04-09 18:24:25',	'2016-04-10 01:24:25',	'no I haven\'t. when dealing with that register it\'s possible to play just about any note with variations because we\'re dealing with overtones but to ensure pitch it\'s best to go with the more proven ones. I lay out 2 for every note. just in case someone has an easier time with one of them. I rarely play those notes using a different fingering.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
(229405,	7804,	'Ade',	'indicajak@gmail.com',	'',	'82.45.62.33',	'2016-08-29 10:18:39',	'2016-08-29 17:18:39',	'Its all dependant on the horn. I\'m getting by for now on a Rumanian Arta Guban. G3 just pops out time after time. I\'ve had Conn\'s, Kings, Selmers on which I have had to really struggle. Nothing worse than hitting G3...and zilch. Doesn\'t look good.',	0,	'1',	'',	'',	0,	0),
