(1046,	321,	'_wp_attachment_image_alt',	'Johnny Ferreira'),
(1047,	270,	'Notes',	'MAKE SURE TO TELL MATT OTTO, PETE THOMAS, JOHNNY FERREIRA, AND JEFF DRISKILL ONCE THIS IS LIVE\r\n\r\nJOHNNY FERRIERA\r\n\r\n- Assuming that not doubling was a conscious choice, why did you make that decision?\r\n\r\nAlthough I truly love the sound of the clarinet ( or the \"cane of pain\" as some of my friends call it!) I even gave lessons on it a long time ago when I spent some time teaching before my recording and touring career took off. Truth is I never felt comfortable playing it. Somewhat with the fingering, but mostly it was the embouchure that I didn\'t fall for. Probably because it\'s so different than the sax.\r\n\r\nI tried playing the flute in an early band I was in.. it was the disco era so there was always a hit song with a flute in it but playing flute always made me a bit dizzy.. probably improper breathing but I never really took to it.\r\n\r\nI get along pretty well on the keyboard since as a kid I took accordion lessons. Since I always played in rock, blues and R&B groups there was always an opportunity to lend a hand comping on a synth... this went a long way in these types of bands.. especially if some of the songs weren\'t suited for a horn.\r\n\r\n- How much (if any) work have you had to turn down not doubling? What types of gigs?\r\n\r\nI have never turned down a session cause people know me as a sax player and call me for that. I normally do sessions on tenor of course but have also done some on soprano and bari. The bari is a natural cause as a single player you can lay down a tenor track or 2 then add a bri and you have a nice sax section just like that.\r\n\r\n- Would you recommend that aspiring professional sax players who dislike playing flute and clarinet skip the whole doubling thing?\r\n\r\nWell, that all depends on the genre a player is involved in... If you\'re in a more traditional big band or orchestra type thing you will get some charts that require doubling and some of these expect a saxophonist to be proficient in clarinet and/or flute.\r\n\r\nIf like me, you;re in a more pop, rock, or blues kind of thing and you don\'t particularily like those instruments then piano or any type of keyboard comes in real handy... believe me, a band that wants a sax player will be even more exited to learn that if that player can also do some keyboard comping to help out their groove, all the better for them.\r\n\r\nEnter Matt Otto.\r\n\r\n1) In the past, did you play gigs where you had to double?\r\nI have only played tenor and soprano saxophone (and a little alto in the 80\'s) so I\'ve not had any opportunities to gig on any other instruments.\r\n\r\n2) Assuming that not doubling was a conscious choice, why did you make that decision?\r\nI wanted to play saxophone as a child. I was never very interested in the other instruments. If I had to do it over I\'d play bass, which I feel suites my personality best.\r\n\r\n3) What types of gigs do you find yourself playing these days? (ie: jazz quartet, rock bands, etc)\r\nI play mostly small group jazz gigs, trio, quartet, quintet, sextet etc..... mostly gigs where I improvise a lot.\r\n\r\n4) How much work have you had to turn down not doubling? What types of gigs?\r\nI\'ve only turned down big band work over the years, although I did play in Ed Neumeisters Big Band in LA, you can hear me on a few solos on his new big band album, \"ENJO live at the Jazz Bakery\"...his lead chair has no doubles, at least the songs we recorded.\r\n\r\n5) Would you recommend that aspiring professional sax players who dislike playing flute and clarinet skip the whole doubling thing?\r\nI think a player should do what he or she wants to do in music. Jazz doesn\'t pay much, if you\'re doing it for the money you might want to try something else. If your doing it for your love of music and art just play the instruments you love.\r\n\r\n6) Would you double if it was the right gig music-wise or money-wise?\r\nFor me, it\'s not a matter of money or the right gig, It\'s just like asking me to drive the space shuttle, I just don\'t know how.\r\n\r\n7) MATT OTTO Is there anything you\'d like to promote that I can include at the end of this article (new album, web site, etc)?\r\nI offer a lot of my recorded albums for free on my blog \"mattotto.org\" as well as improvisation instruction. The rest of my albums are available on CD baby or itunes.'),
