(522,	1,	'2010-11-05 08:00:10',	'2010-11-05 15:00:10',	'<img class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-366\" title=\"the_week_in_saxophone\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/the_week_in_saxophone.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"628\" height=\"263\" />\r\n\r\n<strong>Your weekly sampling of some of this past week\'s saxophone-related news:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"http://www.signonsandiego.com/news/2010/nov/02/jazz-legend-james-moody-battling-cancer/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jazz legend James Moody battling cancer</a></li>\r\n 	<li>Stars of saxophone to hit Istanbul stage (Lovano, Liebman, Coltrane) [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/arts/bradford-marsalis-adds-mood-to-fred-schepisis-storm/story-e6frg8n6-1225945809739\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branford Marsalis adds mood to Fred Schepisi\'s Storm</a></li>\r\n 	<li>The great Gato Barbieri again purrs in Santo Domingo [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/arts/battle-lines/story-e6frg8n6-1225946544078\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Battle lines</a> (Femi Kuti)</li>\r\n 	<li>Doug Webb – Midnight (2010, Positone) [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.press-citizen.com/article/20101101/NEWS01/11010306/Sax-playing-leads-to-NPR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sax playing leads to NPR</a></li>\r\n 	<li>All About Jazz reviews Scurvy’s Fracture [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.jazzwisemagazine.com/component/content/article/67-2010/11665-jazz-breaking-news-matana-roberts-to-play-ljf-show-and-to-release-live-album-in-early-2011\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Matana Roberts To Play LJF Show And To Release Live Album In Early-2011</a></li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://coldjazz.blogspot.com/2010/11/jeff-coffin-interview.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jeff Coffin Interview</a></li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.guardian.co.uk/music/2010/oct/31/trish-clowes-tangent-jazz-review\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Trish Clowes: Tangent – review</a></li>\r\n 	<li>Professor\'s works to be presented at recital [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://saxstation.com/saxophone-transcription-and-the-quadratic-formula.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Saxophone, Transcription, and the Quadratic Formula</a></li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.eddierichmusic.com/?p=549\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"> 2010 Adolphe Sax International Competition</a></li>\r\n 	<li>Update: Saxophone National Anthems (iTunes App) [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]</li>\r\n</ul>',	'The Week in Saxophone: Nov 5, 2010',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'the-week-in-saxophone-nov-5-2010',	'',	'https://www.johnnybutler.com/archives/205\nhttps://saxstation.com/saxophone-transcription-and-the-quadratic-formula.htm\nhttps://www.eddierichmusic.com/?p=549',	'2018-06-25 21:20:46',	'2018-06-26 03:20:46',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=522',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
(538,	1,	'2011-01-05 08:00:15',	'2011-01-05 16:00:15',	'<strong><img class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-1226\" title=\"80s-sax-solos\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/80s-sax-solos.jpg\" alt=\"80\'s Sax Solos\" width=\"628\" height=\"223\" />The popular music of the 1980\'s carried with it a powerful legacy for us saxophone players. </strong>\r\n\r\nUnlike the pop music of today, much of which is sampled, sequenced, and looped, the music of <strong>the 80\'s made frequent use of the saxophone solo</strong>. Classic solos on songs such as \"Careless Whisper,\" \"I Can\'t Go for That,\"  and \"Caribbean Queen\" helped to plant the saxophone smack-dab into the musical mainstream.\r\n\r\nOf course, some of these solos were downright lame, but there were a few gems such as Michael Brecker\'s solo on Donald Fagan\'s ballad, \"Maxine.\"\r\n\r\nBut let\'s face it - <strong>most of these solos fell into the \"downright lame\" category. </strong>\r\n\r\nFor those of us saxfolk who can appreciate the pop sax solos of 80\'s, I\'ve come across a website - or rather, a web-<em>page</em> just for you. On this page, you\'ll find an exhaustive list of that decade\'s signature solos, each solo complete with audio sample, letter grade, brief commentary, and a variety of grading criteria such as <strong>\"Vibrato Overuse,\" \"Middle School Level,\" and \"Good Continuity\"</strong> to name just a few.\r\n<h4>To vist the site and quench your rabid hunger for sax solo action from the era of Ronald Reagan and feathered hair, <a href=\"http://imacomputa.org/sax\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">click yourself on over here</a>.</h4>',	'80\'s Sax Solos',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'80s-sax-solos',	'',	'',	'2018-06-19 11:55:14',	'2018-06-19 17:55:14',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=538',	0,	'post',	'',	12,	0),
(548,	1,	'2010-11-22 08:00:51',	'2010-11-22 16:00:51',	'<a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/playing-sax-clean.jpg\"><img class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-594\" title=\"playing-sax-clean\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/playing-sax-clean.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"628\" height=\"228\" /></a>\r\n\r\nOne of the hallmarks of any professional musician is the clarity and evenness of the individual notes they play as part of a note-packed passage of music, such as a 16th or 32nd note run.\r\n\r\nI recently came across a helpful discovery in this area while practicing my chromatic scale.\r\n<h2>Cleanliness is Next to Godliness</h2>\r\nAchieving that  level of cleanliness is something that comes with years of practice, but I\'ve found one concept that had an<strong> immediate result</strong> for the better in my technique<strong>. </strong>\r\n\r\n<strong>I started listening for the \"click\" of each individual key or key combination as a </strong><em>gentle</em><strong> percussion hit as played by my fingers.</strong> The click-click of the keys coming down during, let\'s say, a chromatic scale, can be perceived as though it were a perfectly even 16th or 32nd-note snare drum roll.\r\n\r\nI describe the percussive effect here as <em>gentle</em> because if I were to think of the individual notes as being overly percussive in nature, then that would mean I\'m using too much energy to achieve the nimbleness necessary to get all of those notes out with <strong>solid and deliberate-sounding precision</strong>.\r\n\r\nUltimately, whenever I\'m playing <em>anything</em>, my goal is to <strong>embody the complete musical picture</strong> (melody, harmony, rhythm, tone quality, feeling, etc), but this is just another way to shake up my perception and get those fingers following the ears.\r\n<h2>Attain Virtuosity While Enjoying the <em>Jersey Shore</em></h2>\r\nSome say real virtuosi are too busy practicing to watch TV (crazy talk, I tell ya). Practicing the fingering of scales, arpeggios, etc with perfect timing is indeed something you could, hypothetically, do while <strong>watching TV</strong>. As long as you hear those keys clicking down in <strong><em>clean and solid</em> 8th or 16th-note rhythms </strong>with each note coming out as a <strong>singular click</strong> (as opposed to a sloppy flam of multiple keys coming down a few milliseconds apart from eachother), then this pastime, sub-optimal as it is, would probably <strong>help more than it would hurt</strong>.\r\n<h2>Only Try This at Home</h2>\r\nWhile practicing a percussive approach to saxophone technique may work for exercises at home, I\'ve found that focusing on the rhythmic accuracy of the key-clicks while improvising is a massive distraction. The point here is to develop some solid muscle memory so that we can let loose while we\'re being creative.\r\n<h2>Let\'s Practice</h2>\r\nMy former piano teacher once showed me an exercise that really applies to any instrument, and it involves modifying the rhythm of scales and other note-filled passages. I could try and explain it, but suffice to say that practicing this with one\'s ear focused on the rhythmic clicking of the keys could be very helpful.\r\n<h4><a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/Clean-Playing-Exercise.pdf\"><strong>Download Sheet Music for Clean Playing Exercise (PDF)</strong></a></h4>\r\n<strong>In Action: </strong>Johnny Griffin, Champion of Clean Playing (Johnny\'s solo at 3:48).\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/reqsR0HHz7s',	'How to Play Fast and Clean',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'how-to-play-fast-and-clean',	'',	'',	'2013-08-25 11:25:32',	'2013-08-25 18:25:32',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=548',	0,	'post',	'',	5,	0),
(552,	1,	'2010-11-08 12:49:07',	'2010-11-08 20:49:07',	'<img class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-554\" title=\"dave-liebman-big-band\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/dave-liebman-big-band.jpg\" alt=\"Dave Liebman Big Band\" width=\"628\" height=\"248\" />\r\n\r\nDave Liebman apparently has a big band - who knew? Very cool to know though, as his concert late last month was hailed by AllAboutJazz.com\'s Victor L. Schermer as comparable to \"comparison with the classic <strong>Thelonious Monk Big Band</strong> at Town Hall in 1959\" Although it was a big band setting with great <strong>arrangements by the likes of Vince Mendoza, Tim Hagans, and Jim McNeely,</strong> the group had the lightness and feel of a small group.\r\n\r\nOn top of all of that, Vic Juris, one of my mentors from William Paterson University\'s jazz program was heavily featured as well, so it\'s a small world after all.\r\n\r\nBesides Dave, sax players on the gig included Gunnar Mossblad: director, alto and soprano saxophone, Piccolo, flute, clarinet; Charles Pillow: alto saxophone, oboe, flute; Dave Riekenberg: tenor saxophone, flute, clarinet; Tim Ries: tenor saxophone, clarinet; Jay Brandford: baritone saxophone, bass clarinet.\r\n\r\n<a href=\"https://www.allaboutjazz.com/php/article.php?id=38002&amp;pg=1\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Click here to read the whole shebang.</strong></a>',	'Dave Liebman Big Band In Philadelphia',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'dave-liebman-big-band-in-philadelphia',	'',	'',	'2010-11-08 12:49:07',	'2010-11-08 20:49:07',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=552',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
(554,	1,	'2010-11-08 12:42:02',	'2010-11-08 20:42:02',	'',	'dave-liebman-big-band',	'',	'inherit',	'open',	'open',	'',	'dave-liebman-big-band',	'',	'',	'2010-11-08 12:42:02',	'2010-11-08 20:42:02',	'',	552,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/dave-liebman-big-band.jpg',	0,	'attachment',	'image/jpeg',	0,	0),
(561,	1,	'2010-11-23 08:00:52',	'2010-11-23 16:00:52',	'Every once in a while I come across a video that\'s so amazing and inspiring that I just have to share it. <strong>This video of Chris Potter playing Charlie Parker\'s confirmation sans accompaniment is more certainly one of those videos.</strong>\r\n\r\nThe video is actually a part of a masterclass DVD series sold by Roberto\'s Winds, and you can pick up the entire set <a href=\"https://www.robertoswinds.com/collections/accessories-cds-dvds\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">here</a>.\r\n\r\nWhat I love about this excerpt is that it demonstrates the multitude of dimensions present in Chris\' playing. Besides truly having a beginning, a middle, and an end, the solo features utterly creative phrasing, and takes the listener on a journey through the <strong>entire history of jazz saxophone</strong>.\r\n\r\n<strong>I defy you to watch the video and not be totally blown away and inspired.</strong> (...or depressed).\r\n\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-B-2EtfKVlo',	'Mind-Melting Chris Potter Acapella Performance',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'mind-melting-chris-potter-video',	'',	'',	'2020-06-27 07:42:48',	'2020-06-27 12:42:48',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=561',	0,	'post',	'',	2,	0),
(565,	1,	'2010-11-09 00:10:07',	'2010-11-09 08:10:07',	'',	'Clean Playing Exercise',	'',	'inherit',	'open',	'open',	'',	'clean-playing-exercise',	'',	'',	'2010-11-09 00:10:07',	'2010-11-09 08:10:07',	'',	548,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/Clean-Playing-Exercise.pdf',	0,	'attachment',	'application/pdf',	0,	0),
(570,	1,	'2010-11-10 08:00:54',	'2010-11-10 16:00:54',	'I know that this brilliant video by sax player <strong>Allen Mezquida</strong> has been out for a while, but I still wanted to feature it here in case anyone missed it.\r\n\r\nWhat I love about it is not only is the animation awesome, but<strong> the saxophone playing is great</strong>. Amazing to know that Allen was responsible for <em style=\"font-weight: bold;\">both </em>the animation and the music.\r\n\r\nTo see the entire series of jazz-inspired videos known as \"Smigly\", check out the website at https://smigly.tv. [Sorry(!), but this web page has disappeared since the original publication of this article]\r\n\r\nI plan on featuring more of Allen\'s unique work in the future, as there is a very interesting back story as to how he combined his talents for music and animation. But for now, I leave you with this lovely video entitled \"I Heart Jazz.\"\r\n\r\n<object width=\"560\" height=\"340\" classid=\"clsid:d27cdb6e-ae6d-11cf-96b8-444553540000\" codebase=\"https://download.macromedia.com/pub/shockwave/cabs/flash/swflash.cab#version=6,0,40,0\"><param name=\"allowFullScreen\" value=\"true\" /><param name=\"allowscriptaccess\" value=\"always\" /><param name=\"src\" value=\"https://www.youtube.com/v/AcwZtodnnUs?fs=1&amp;hl=en_US\" /><param name=\"allowfullscreen\" value=\"true\" /><embed type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"560\" height=\"340\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/v/AcwZtodnnUs?fs=1&amp;hl=en_US\" allowscriptaccess=\"always\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" /></object>',	'Cartoon with Killer Alto Saxophone Solo',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'cartoon-with-killer-alto-saxophone-solo',	'',	'',	'2018-06-25 22:27:08',	'2018-06-26 04:27:08',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=570',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
