(1729,	1,	'2011-02-23 07:00:06',	'2011-02-23 15:00:06',	'<img class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-1741\" title=\"virtual-sax\" src=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//wp-content/uploads/virtual-sax.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"628\" height=\"312\" />\r\n\r\nThanks to accomplished UK sax man <strong>Ben Castle,</strong> I recently came across what\'s become a fabulous way to suck away precious hours of my life.  It\'s called <strong>\"Virtual Sax!\"</strong> and <strong>it allows any of us to put together a <em>funkyjazzy</em> solo by merely moving our mouse</strong> in rhythm to two different pre-recorded backing tracks.\r\n<h2>How this Works</h2>\r\nIt\'s quite simple really. First you choose which of the <strong>two different backing tracks</strong> you\'d like to play along with. From there, you move the mouse over the keys on the goofy-looking virtual saxophone. <strong>Most of the keys trigger great sax licks</strong> (played by Ben himself, I presume) while the rest of the keys on the upper left-hand site of the horn trigger <strong>individual notes</strong> for those looking for a more expansive means of virtual self expression.\r\n\r\nThis is especially great for those of us working on laptop or notebook computers where one can enjoy the benefits of the <strong>trackpad</strong>, since that trackpad works much better than the mouse for these type of shenanigans.\r\n\r\nAnyhow, I can  yammer about this all I want, but the best thing to do is <a href=\"https://www.bencastle.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>hop on over to Ben Castle\'s site and take it for a spin! </strong></a>',	'If you Can Move your Mouse you Can Burn on Tenor',	'',	'publish',	'open',	'open',	'',	'if-you-can-move-your-mouse-you-can-burn-on-tenor',	'',	'',	'2018-06-27 13:01:25',	'2018-06-27 19:01:25',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=1729',	0,	'post',	'',	1,	0),
(1738,	1,	'2011-03-20 21:17:40',	'0000-00-00 00:00:00',	'For over 20 years now, saxophonist, composer, producer educator, and record label owner Greg Osby has been making history as one of jazz music\'s most distinctive voices. Landing in New York by way of St. Louis, Washington D.C., and Boston, Osby has been the toast of the town nabbing numerous awards and critical acclaim for his recordings and powerful live performances.\n\nBesides expanding his musical vocabulary playing with various ethnic and new music ensembles in NYC, Greg has played alongside masters such as Herbie Hancock, Dizzy Gillespie, Jack DeJohnette, Andrew Hill, Muhal Richard Abrams, Jim Hall and Jaki Byard. 1985 found Greg joining legendary drummer Jack DeJohnette\'s innovative and acclaimed group \"Special Edition\" which helped to radically shape Greg\'s richly unique approach to music.\n\nBeginning his solo recording career in 1987 on German label, JMT, Greg joined the legendary roster of artists on Blue Note records in 1990. Today, Greg features many of jazz music\'s best and brightest up and coming musicians on his own label, \"Inner Circle Music.\"\n<ol>\n	<li>What was your main inspiration for making music your profession?</li>\n	<li>Anyone who\'s ever heard you play knows that you have a very distinctive approach to the alto sax on the instrument. Was coming up with an original voice on the instrument something that you worked at, or did that simply happen automatically as a byproduct of your overall musical development?</li>\n	<li>The music you play in your solo projects is very unique with influences such as avante garde jazz and rock. Can you talk about how your vision</li>\n	<li>You\'re very well known as a composer - can you talk a bit about your compositional process?</li>\n	<li>It\'s clear from your playing that you have a deep sense of harmony. Can you share some tips for sax players to improve their harmonic vocabulary?</li>\n	<li>Many of your compositions are in odd meters. Can you talk a little bit about how you became comfortable playing and hearing odd-metered music?</li>\n	<li>You\'ve served as a judge for the extremely prestigious Thelonious Monk International Jazz Competition. What are some of the things you listen for in an up and coming saxophonist?</li>\n	<li>What do you currently find yourself practicing most?</li>\n	<li>Musically speaking, what has been exciting you lately?</li>\n	<li>To someone new to your work, are there any recordings you suggest they start with?</li>\n</ol>',	'Greg Osby Interview',	'',	'draft',	'open',	'open',	'',	'',	'',	'',	'2011-03-20 21:17:40',	'2011-03-21 04:17:40',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=1738',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
