(4161,	1,	'2013-07-31 21:46:16',	'0000-00-00 00:00:00',	'Hello Mike,\n\nI would say breathe only through your nose when practicing long tones, so you still get your breaths in, but you\'re not letting your embouchure rest.\n\nThere\'s also some great tips and exercises in this article:\nhttps://www.myspace.com/randyhunterjazz/blog/315545037\n\nThe key is to make sure you take many breaks, you don\'t want to overdo it and then really have problems, so when you feel your chops getting really tired, give it a rest, for at least a minute or two.\n\nIdeally, you should be playing with a very loose embouchure so that the reed is actually vibrating and not being constricted.\n\nI hope that helps!\n\nDoron Orenstein\nBest. Saxophone. Website. Ever.\nhttps://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local/\nhttps://www.BulletproofSaxophonePlaying.com\n323.252.3122\n\nNewsletter: https://eepurl.com/bcvb-\n\nOn Fri, Dec 28, 2012 at 5:14 PM, Mike Krechevsky &lt;krechevskym@msn.com&gt; wrote:\nFrom: Mike Krechevsky &lt;krechevskym@msn.com&gt;\nSubject: Embroschure Exercise\n\nMessage Body:\nFirst: Great Site. Very accessable. My question/observation: I recently picked up a HarryHartmann carbon reed with a strength of hard. I was playing a MH before. While a bit difficult what I found was after a few days when I went back to the MH altisimo was much more accessable. Primary mpc PNR Guardala Super King on a 1960 King Silversonic.\n\nI know for overall physical conditioning it\'s good to mix it up (Running: Distance &amp; Sprints; Weights: Heavy &amp;Light;etc) Thoughts about \"embroschure conditioning\"?\n\n--\nThis mail is sent via contact form on Best. Saxophone. Website. Ever. https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local/',	'Embouchure exercises',	'',	'draft',	'open',	'open',	'',	'',	'',	'',	'2013-07-31 21:46:16',	'2013-08-01 04:46:16',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local//?p=4161',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
