(7454,	1,	'2020-02-20 15:58:36',	'0000-00-00 00:00:00',	'There was a time where you couldn\'t go five minutes without hearing that distinct sax sound. For those of you who weren\'t born yet, the songs <em>Careless Whisper</em> or <em>Baker Street</em> may mean nothing, but for an entire generation, these songs represent the sound of the 80\'s when talking about pop music.\r\n\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=izGwDsrQ1eQ\r\n\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fo6aKnRnBxM\r\n\r\nThese songs were consistently at the top of the charts, <a href=\"https://www.officialcharts.com/search/singles/careless-whisper/\">with <em>Careless Whisper</em> staying at number one for a staggering 23 weeks</a>. Today, the saxophone isn\'t as prominent in contemporary music. Sure, you hear it every so often outside of the \"jazz bubble\", but it\'s not where it used to be in the public consciousness.\r\n\r\nHowever, that doesn\'t mean that things should stay the way they are. I\'ve put together a list of five reasons why the saxophone solo should be back in popular music, just like it was in the 1980s. Read on to know more about why we need to bring the saxophone back into Top 40 charts!\r\n<h2>The Best Ones <em>(in my humble opinion)</em></h2>\r\nGiven the target audience of this website, chances are that the two solos I just featured are probably not your faves. I\'m guessing that solos such as Phil Woods\' solo over Billy Joel\'s \"Just the Way You Are\" from 1977 and Michael Brecker\'s solo over Cameo\'s \"Candy\" from 1978 would be more your speed (not to mention other classic Brecker solos such as James Taylor\'s \"<a href=\"https://youtu.be/XTh1335y7EA\">Don\'t Let Me Be Lonely Tonight</a>\" and Cameo\'s \"<a href=\"https://youtu.be/sn8KYD1Vco0\">Candy</a>\").\r\n\r\nhttps://youtu.be/HaA3YZ6QdJU?t=176\r\n\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3saecjSNTlM\r\n<h2>The Case for The Return of the Sax Solo in Pop Music</h2>\r\n<h3>Reason #1: The Saxophone Solo is Already Being Reintroduced by Major Artists</h3>\r\nWho would have ever thunk it - Kanye West with Kenny G - but lo and behold - this unlikely collaboration is indeed a reality with \"Use This Gospel\" taken off of Kanye\'s latest album, <em>JESUS IS KING</em>.\r\n.\r\n\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8yQVcGkbpAc\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h3>Reason #2: Jazz is Getting Cool Again</h3>\r\nAnother reason to bring back the sax solo is the recent resurgence of jazz. <a href=\"https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/whats-behind-the-new-jazz-resurgence-703853/\">Jazz critic Nate Chinen tells Rolling Stone that</a>, \"I feel like there are more reasons to be excited about improvised music today than at any time during my 41 years on the planet” — emphasizing that we may very well just be on the verge of another golden age for the genre. But it isn\'t just talent that\'s brought about this newfound popularity. Jazz has also been pushed back into popular culture thanks to people like Jon Batiste, best known as the bandleader on The Late Show with Stephen Colbert.\r\n\r\nIndeed, this visibility, along with the growing number of new talents has reintroduced jazz to this generation. It\'s not as if jazz stopped being good, it\'s just that people needed to be reminded of how good it could really be.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<strong>Use in Hip Hop</strong>\r\n\r\nIMAGE: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Keith_Jarrett.jpg\r\n\r\nWe\'ve written about why it\'s important to trace music back to its roots in <a href=\"https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local/why-its-critical-to-know-the-influences-of-your-influences/\">our feature on <em>Why It’s Critical to Know the Influences of Your Influences</em></a>, bringing us to our first reason to bring the sax back. The saxophone is an instrument that has significantly influenced the world of jazz, and in turn, has shaped hip hop as we know it today. Shades of jazz\'s influence on hip hop can still be seen in the form of samples.\r\n\r\n<a href=\"https://pitchfork.com/features/from-the-pitchfork-review/9869-two-turntables-and-a-saxophone/\">Hip hop artists like Anenon have all sampled jazz greats such as Keith Jarrett</a>, bringing their music to a newer audience. The success of these tracks is a testament to just how successful sax players can be in contemporary music.\r\n\r\n<strong>Presence in Contemporary Music</strong>\r\n\r\nIMAGE: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fkj_presspic.jpg\r\n\r\nBecause pop music is so saturated with electronic sounds nowadays, adding a short 8-bar or 4-bar sax solo would definitely add value to tracks in terms of freshness and originality. To jump off from the previous point, there have been several musicians that have brought the saxophone to the present, not just via samples but through the direct use of the instrument itself. A notable artist who has done this is FKJ. Famous for his use of the electronic looper, <a href=\"https://www.tonebox.com/pedal/electro-harmonix-nano-pog\">his use of the Electro-Harmonix Nano POG to create hauntingly divine tones</a> and his multi-instrumental virtuosity, FKJ also known as French Kiwi Juice has introduced the sax to countless Gen Z audiophiles. Indeed, the use of technology has reinvigorated the saxophone and has made it more attuned to the listening tastes of the younger generation.\r\n\r\nFKJ reintroduced the instrument in one of the most stellar ways possible as he included it in one of his most iconic performances to date. <a href=\"https://www.wbgo.org/post/fkj-one-man-orchestra-and-internet-phenom-his-jazz-roots-and-concert-clouds#stream/0\">FKJ\'s intimate concert at Salar de Uyuni</a>, which is one of the world\'s largest salt flats, featured two saxophones seamlessly blended in with his modern sound.\r\n\r\n<strong>Popularity of the Sax Solo</strong>\r\n\r\nIMAGE: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_Gaga_interview_2016.jpg\r\n\r\nMusicians of varying genres have utilized the sax solo in many songs. <a href=\"https://slate.com/culture/2011/05/what-s-the-best-sax-solo-in-pop-music-history.html\">Artists like Lady Gaga have used the sax solo prominently in their material</a>, most recently in her album <em>Born This Way</em>.\r\n\r\nThe sustained popularity of this demonstration of technical brilliance is enough of a reason to shine the spotlight on the saxophone. It bears the same effect as the guitar solos that rock stars utilize to wow crowds all over the globe – a testament to the potential that the instrument has for mainstream appeal.\r\n\r\n<strong>Resurgence of Jazz</strong>\r\n\r\nIMAGE: https://www.pexels.com/photo/two-men-blowing-air-instruments-1365167/\r\n\r\nAnother reason to bring back the sax is the recent resurgence of jazz. <a href=\"https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/whats-behind-the-new-jazz-resurgence-703853/\">Jazz critic Nate Chinen tells Rolling Stone that</a>, \"I feel like there are more reasons to be excited about improvised music today than at any time during my 41 years on the planet” — emphasizing that we may very well just be on the verge of another golden age for the genre. But it isn\'t just talent that\'s brought about this newfound popularity. Jazz has also been pushed back into popular culture thanks to people like Jon Batiste, best known as the bandleader on The Late Show with Stephen Colbert.\r\n\r\nIndeed, this visibility, along with the growing number of new talents has reintroduced jazz to this generation. It\'s not as if jazz stopped being good, it\'s just that people needed to be reminded of how good it could really be.\r\n\r\n<strong>Rising New Talent</strong>\r\n\r\nIMAGE: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Kamasi_Washington_(2015).jpg\r\n\r\nLastly, the amount of new talent should be enough to push the saxophone back into the mainstream. No other artist is a better example of this than Kamasi Washington. Washington has played alongside a diverse and heralded group both old and new. However, what cemented Washington\'s place in the pantheon of modern music is his work on Pulitzer Prize winner Kendrick Lamar\'s <em>To Pimp a Butterfly</em>.\r\n\r\nThere is no questioning that <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/music/2019/nov/21/fresh-heirs-how-kamasi-washington-gave-jazz-back-to-the-kids\">Washington is reintroducing the saxophone</a>, and by extension, jazz, to the audiences of today. Washington and the other modern talents are a good indication for the future of the saxophone – a future that is getting brighter by the day.',	'5 Reasons to Bring the Saxophone Solo Back Into Top 40 Music',	'',	'draft',	'open',	'open',	'',	'',	'',	'',	'2020-06-27 07:42:48',	'2020-06-27 12:42:48',	'',	0,	'https://bestsaxophonewebsiteevercom.local/?p=7454',	0,	'post',	'',	0,	0),
